\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Lawsuit From Mississippi Is More Direct Challenge To Roe v. Wade Precedent\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adeel Hassan \par \uc2 1,441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A case coming before the Supreme Court is seen as potentially pivotal in establishing how aggressively the justices might move to place new constraints on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In December, the Supreme Court will hear arguments on a Mississippi law that is a direct challenge to Roe v. Wade, setting the stage for what could be the most consequential \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ruling in decades.\par \par \uc2 The addition of Justice Amy Coney Barrett to the court in 2020 has strengthened the court's conservative majority and energized the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, which has long sought to overturn Roe, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and barred states from banning the procedure before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 Here's a guide to understanding what is in the law and what is at stake in the case:\par \par \uc2 What is in the Mississippi \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law?\par \par \uc2 The Mississippi law that will be reviewed by the court makes most abortions illegal after 15 weeks of pregnancy, about two months earlier than Roe and later decisions allow. Most experts estimate fetal viability to be about 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The law was enacted in 2018 by the Republican-dominated Mississippi Legislature but never went into effect because of an immediate legal challenge that led to a federal appellate court blocking its enforcement.\par \par \uc2 The law bans abortions if ''the probable gestational age of the unborn human'' is determined to be more than 15 weeks, with narrow exceptions for medical emergencies or ''a severe fetal abnormality.''\par \par \uc2 The state says that the fetus has made important physiological developments by 15 weeks, and that abortions are riskier to the mother at this stage of pregnancy. The Jackson Women's Health Organization, which as the sole \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Mississippi is at the center of the case, gave evidence to a federal appeals court showing that fetal viability is impossible at 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 A federal appeals court said the state had not shown any medical evidence proving otherwise, affirming a lower-court ruling blocking the law and essentially saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a right until a fetus is considered viable. If the law passed in 2018 were in practice, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who violated it could have their medical license suspended or revoked.\par \par \uc2 Restricted by several state laws, Jackson Women's Health Organization already does not perform abortions after 16 weeks.\par \par \uc2 More than 93 percent of abortions in Mississippi in 2018 were performed before 14 weeks of gestation, and 75 percent were before 10 weeks, according to the most recent data from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why is this case considered so important?\par \par \uc2 In recent years, Republican-controlled states have passed similar legislation only to have the laws struck down in appeals courts because they were in conflict with the precedent created by the Supreme Court. Those states, in effect, were vying for the chance to be heard by the court, especially after the retirement in 2018 of Justice \uc2 Anthony M. Kennedy\uc2 , a cautious supporter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi's appeal of the lower court ruling sat on the Supreme Court's docket since the fall of 2020, about one month before Justice Barrett was confirmed to replace Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , who had died.\par \par \uc2 In May, the court, with its new 6-to-3 conservative majority, agreed to hear the Mississippi case, known as Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization. (The state health officer, Thomas E. Dobbs, is the named petitioner in the case.)\par \par \uc2 In its brief to the Supreme Court, Mississippi asked the justices to overrule Roe and the court's 1992 decision in Planned Parenthood v. Casey, which said states could not impose an ''undue burden'' on the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 The court probably will not rule on the Mississippi case until June. If the justices were to approve the law, Roe's viability standard would no longer be the law of the land. The Supreme Court has reversed its own positions on various legal issues dozens of times, including several in the 16 years since Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . was appointed in 2005.\par \par \uc2 How does this law compare with Texas' new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law?\par \par \uc2 A new Texas law bans abortions once cardiac activity can be detected in the embryo. There is no heart at this stage of development, only electrical activity in developing cells that starts at about six weeks, before many women are aware they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law also, and crucially, forbids the state to enforce it. Instead, citizens are incentivized with suing anyone who performs abortions, or ''aids and abets'' them.\par \par \uc2 This unique formulation makes the law very hard to challenge in court because there is no single entity responsible for enforcement. Effectively, nearly all abortions in the state have been banned since the law went into effect.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court refused to immediately block the law but in an unusual move, the justices agreed to fast-track their consideration of appeals from the Justice Department and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas, scheduling arguments for Nov. 1.\par \par \uc2 The Mississippi law does not use the citizen-enforcement approach and would be enforced by the state.\par \par \uc2 How likely is it that the court will overturn Roe?\par \par \uc2 Many experts say that Roe faces its stiffest test, and that it is in peril. ''Change is about to come with regard to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Elizabeth W. Sepper, a law professor at the University of Texas at Austin, recently told The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . ''I think they're going to overrule Roe v. Wade.''\par \par \uc2 Many experts believe that the Supreme Court would not wait for a year to take the case only to strike down the Mississippi law and simply reaffirm what lower courts have already been doing in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases for a long time. The justices have turned down other state appeals over pre-viability \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 It could also focus on other cases, like Casey, and find that the 15-week cutoff does not place an ''undue burden'' on those seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Such a ruling arguably would not represent a wholesale overturning of Roe.\par \par \uc2 But if the Mississippi law stands, the ruling could give states greater latitude to limit how and when abortions are performed.\par \par \uc2 What could happen after the ruling?\par \par \uc2 If the court moves to overturn Roe, about one dozen Republican-controlled states already have so-called trigger laws that would make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlawful almost immediately. And more could follow quickly.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, for example, Gov. Greg Abbott, a Republican, signed a law in June that says nearly all abortions would become illegal in the state 30 days later if Roe is ever overturned. Doctors who then performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  there could be sentenced to life in prison.\par \par \uc2 Conversely, 15 states and the District of Columbia have laws to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights without Roe. Their policies usually bar the government from interfering with the right to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability, or to protect the health of the pregnant woman.\par \par \uc2 What do the law's supporters and opponents say?\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Lynn Fitch of Mississippi, who is the state's first female attorney general and the first Republican to hold the office since Reconstruction, acknowledges that she is seeking to overturn Supreme Court precedent. Ms. Fitch argues that while the justices have allowed state officials to protect women's health and defend lives, they continue to prevent them from enacting laws on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''In these last 50 years, women have carved their own way to achieving a better balance for success in their professional and personal lives,'' Ms. Fitch said in a statement. ''By returning the matter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to state legislatures, we allow a stunted debate on how we support women to flourish. It is time for the court to let go of its hold on this important debate.''\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which represents the Jackson clinic, says that a \u226\'00\'E2\u8364\'20\'AC\u175\'00\'AFreversal of Roe would leave large parts of the South and Midwest without access to legal abortions.\par \par \uc2 Pregnant women who cannot afford to cross state lines to get a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would attempt to end their own pregnancies, the center said, and put them and anyone who helps them subject to prosecution.\par \par \uc2 ''For many, the barriers will simply be too high, and they will be forced to endure the substantial risks of continued pregnancy and childbirth,'' it said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 A man protesting against \uc2  rights was surrounded by a crowd in favor of them in October in Jackson, Miss. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Rory Doyle/Reuters FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211030ehau0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
