\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Texas Urges Top Court Not to Block  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,109 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden administration had asked the court to block the law. State officials called the request procedurally flawed, saying the court was powerless to grant it.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Texas' attorney general urged the Supreme Court on Thursday to leave the state's restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in place, saying that the federal government was not entitled to challenge it.\par \par \uc2 If the justices are nonetheless inclined to hear the Justice Department's request that they block the law while legal challenges proceed, wrote \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , the state's attorney general, they should use the case to overrule Roe v. Wade and to eliminate the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The state law, which has been in effect since Sept. 1, bans most abortions after six weeks and makes no exceptions for pregnancies resulting from rape or incest. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Texas have largely stopped performing the procedure, requiring women seeking abortions to travel out of state.\par \par \uc2 The law, known as Senate Bill 8, was intended to evade review in federal court. It barred state officials from enforcing the law and deputized private individuals to sue anyone who performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or assisted with it in any way, including by providing counseling, financial assistance or a ride to the clinic.\par \par \uc2 Successful plaintiffs are entitled to damages of at least $10,000 and payment of their legal fees. Defendants must bear their own legal fees whether they win or lose.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton wrote that the law's structure tied the justices' hands.\par \par \uc2 ''At bottom, the federal government's complaint is that S.B. 8 is difficult to effectively enjoin,'' Mr. Paxton wrote. ''But there is no requirement that a state write its laws such that they can be easily enjoined.''\par \par \uc2 It would be a dangerous thing, he added, to allow the federal government to challenge state laws to which it objected. ''The United States' lawsuit against Texas is extraordinary in its breadth and consequence, having an impact on precedents that have existed far longer than any right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been recognized,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton contested the Justice Department's assertion that the state law was at odds with Roe v. Wade and Planned Parenthood v. Casey, decisions in which the court said that the Constitution forbids states from banning abortions before fetal viability, or around 22 to 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 ''Merely creating the potential for liability for some abortions is not a ban,'' Mr. Paxton wrote. The only reason many women in Texas cannot obtain abortions, he wrote, ''is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers choose not to provide them because they do not wish to litigate their liability in a state court under a statute they deem unconstitutional.''\par \par \uc2 After devoting most of his brief to procedural arguments about why the Justice Department lacked the ability to sue to block the law, Mr. Paxton took aim at the leading precedents, asking the court to overrule them if it decides to consider the department's application.\par \par \uc2 ''Properly understood, the Constitution does not protect a right to elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' he wrote, adding, ''If it reaches the merits, the court should overturn Roe and Casey.''\par \par \uc2 Last month, in a bitterly divided 5-to-4 decision, the Supreme Court rejected an emergency request from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas to block the law while the providers' legal challenge moved forward. In an unsigned opinion in that separate lawsuit, the majority cited ''complex and novel'' procedural obstacles to blocking the law and stressed that it was not ruling on the constitutionality of the law.\par \par \uc2 The majority wrote that its ruling ''in no way limits other procedurally proper challenges to the Texas law, including in Texas state courts.'' Officials in Texas have said that providers can challenge the law by violating it, getting sued and asserting the law's unconstitutionality as part of their defense.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . joined the court's three more liberal members in dissent.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department then filed its own challenge to the law, one that it said was not subject to the procedural barriers the providers had faced.\par \par \uc2 On Oct. 6, Judge Robert L. Pitman of the Federal District Court in Austin ruled for the federal government, granting it a preliminary injunction forbidding the state, its officials and private individuals acting in concert with them from enforcing the law.\par \par \uc2 ''That other courts may find a way to avoid this conclusion is theirs to decide,'' he wrote. ''This court will not sanction one more day of this offensive deprivation of such an important right.''\par \par \uc2 An injunction was an appropriate response, Judge Pitman wrote, to a law that both violated the Constitution and was drafted to evade judicial review.\par \par \uc2 ''Fully aware that depriving its citizens of this right by direct state action would be flagrantly unconstitutional,'' he wrote, ''the state contrived an unprecedented and transparent statutory scheme to do just that.''\par \par \uc2 A divided three-judge panel of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit, in New Orleans, stayed Judge Pitman's ruling, reinstating the law. The Justice Department then filed an application asking the Supreme Court to lift the stay.\par \par \uc2 In their separate suit, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, after losing a first round in the Supreme Court, filed a new request last month, asking the justices to hear their challenge quickly, bypassing the appeals court, using a procedure called ''certiorari before judgment.''\par \par \uc2 That procedure is rarely used, typically in cases involving national crises like President \uc2 Harry S. Truman\uc2 's seizure of the steel industry and President Richard M. Nixon's refusal to turn over tape recordings to a special prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 The providers said the court should use the procedure to decide what they said was a pressing question: ''whether a state can insulate from federal-court review a law that prohibits the exercise of a constitutional right by delegating to the general public the authority to enforce that prohi\u173\'00\'ADbition through civil actions.''\par \par \uc2 In a second filing on Thursday, Mr. Paxton urged the justices to turn down the providers' request for expedited treatment of their challenge. ''This case does not merit inclusion in the small number of cases where this court has taken the extraordinary step of granting certiorari before judgment,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 In December, the Supreme Court will hear arguments in a challenge to a Mississippi law that bans most abortions after 15 weeks. The case is a direct challenge to Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Protesters in Washington this month. The Texas law was intended to evade review in federal court. (PHOTOGRAPH BY STEFANI REYNOLDS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211022eham00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley