\par\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 All Women Should Support  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Finney Boylan \par \uc2 1,116 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Caitlyn Jenner's run for California governor concluded last month, having earned her a whopping 1 percent of the final votes cast.\par \par \uc2 Her campaign may have gone the way of her short-lived reality series (of which I was a part). But her equivocating response to the new Texas law nearly outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in that state shouldn't soon be forgotten.\par \par \uc2 ''I am for a woman's right to choose,'' she told CNN in September. ''I am also for a state having the ability to make their own laws. So I support Texas in that decision. That's their decision.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Jenner -- and other conservative trans women -- should understand that restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and blocking transgender people's access to health care are two sides of the same coin: a movement to give the state the right to limit what you can or cannot do with your own body.\par \par \uc2 I asked Jules Gill-Peterson, a history professor at Johns Hopkins University and an expert on anti-trans activism, about the connection between these issues. ''Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and anti-trans legislation are products of the same political coalition, using the same scripts and tactics,'' she wrote me. ''In both cases, we see the protection of a fantasized imperiled child justifying heavy-handed police state policies that restrict actual women and children's rights and bodily autonomy. The language of protection, so highly moralized,'' she continued, ''is the perfect alibi for rationalizing widespread harm.''\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, before turning their attention to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Texas legislators attempted to reclassify transgender care for minors as child abuse. In another act, Senate Bill 1311, they tried to prevent physicians from providing gender-affirming care to minors. That act died in the House -- but was not the last attempt to limit trans rights in Texas: This very week, the Legislature heard testimony on House Bill 25, a measure that would limit trans athletes in schools from competing on teams aligned with their gender identity.\par \par \uc2 The author of Senate Bill 1311, Senator Bob Hall, said that his bill ''was being done with love.''\par \par \uc2 Let's be clear: It is not love to force a trans child to go through a puberty that will scar them for the rest of their life. It is not love to force a woman to bear a child against her will. It is not love to deny anyone autonomy over their own body.\par \par \uc2 It is not love to enforce your own ideology upon people who are different from you, simply because you do not understand their experience of being human.\par \par \uc2 Transgender people know this as well as anyone, and not least because of the headwinds we have had to struggle against in our fight to be ourselves. As veterans of this battle, many trans folks are stepping up to advocate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The trans athlete Schuyler Bailar, for example, addressed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights Women's March last weekend in Washington, D.C., noting that, ''This is about all of us.''\par \par \uc2 In many ways, the decision to terminate a pregnancy is not unlike the decision to go through transition: It is a fundamentally private choice that can be made only by the individual in question -- a person who alone knows the truth of their heart, who alone can understand what the consequences of their choices will be in the years to come.\par \par \uc2 Mara Keisling, former head of the National Center for Transgender Equality, told me this week that -- along with the \uc2 Veterans Health Administration\uc2  -- Planned Parenthood is one of the top providers for trans people's health care nationwide. So when conservatives talk about defunding Planned Parenthood, ostensibly because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services it offers, they're also talking about denying a major resource for the well being of trans people.\par \par \uc2 When I read about the seemingly endless efforts to deny people the kind of care they get at these clinics, I wonder what is actually motivating them, at their core. Are anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists really driven by a concern for the ''unborn?'' Is anti-trans sentiment really driven by an understanding of the complex science behind gender variance?\par \par \uc2 Or is it, as I suspect, a fear of difference, a fear of women, or a fear of sex itself?\par \par \uc2 It may also be a fear of nonconformity and difference. A direct consequence of this fear is the harrowing experiences pregnant men and nonbinary people can have in seeking health care. Nick Lloyd, a nonbinary trans person, has described the lack of gender-inclusive language when they tried to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as ''dehumanizing.'' But being different doesn't mean that an individual is less deserving of respectful reproductive services -- including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- than anyone else.\par \par \uc2 Being different simply means that you are human. The only constant in our experience of sex and gender is variation.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, the trans rights movement isn't embraced by all feminists. Over the last decade, a fringe movement of feminists has grown in Britain and the United States, resisting the call for trans rights, claiming that only cisgender women are women. You can call them ''gender critical'' feminists, some go further and call them TERFs (trans-exclusionary radical feminists), or simply people whose hearts -- like the Grinch's -- are two sizes too small.\par \par \uc2 Regardless, the current moment demands that trans women take their rightful place in American feminism. In this fight, everyone believing in the right of all people to control their own bodies ought to be on the same side. Is it so impossible to suggest that we all might look out for one another?\par \par \uc2 On one of the episodes of her reality show, I asked Ms. Jenner how she could support a political party that has so consistently opposed rights for people like us. She replied, ''Every conservative guy out there believes in everybody's rights,'' an assertion I've since had occasion to question in these pages.\par \par \uc2 But if this statement is true, and conservatives do indeed believe in ''everybody's rights,'' then let's see this belief made visible.\par \par \uc2 Surely those rights include the right to make our own choices about our own bodies. Surely those rights include the profound, and simple, gift of being allowed to live our lives in peace.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Ariel Davis FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211011ehab0003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
