\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Federal Panel Restores Texas's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban While It Weighs Appeal\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. David Goodman \par \uc2 707 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A federal appeals court panel temporarily reinstated the law that bans most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy while it considers a district judge's ruling.\par \par \uc2 HOUSTON -- A federal appeals court panel reinstated Texas' restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law late Friday, temporarily restoring a ban on virtually all procedures that had been blocked by a lower court two days earlier in a case brought by the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 The decision by three judges on the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit, in a terse two-page ruling granting a stay as it considers an appeal by the state of Texas, had been expected by many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. While at least six clinics in Texas had begun conducting abortions beyond the limits of the new law this week, most of the state's roughly two dozen providers had opted not to take that step as the case moved through the courts.\par \par \uc2 ''Tonight the Fifth Circuit has granted an administrative stay in the #SB8 case,'' the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on Friday night. ''I will continue to fight to keep #Texas free from federal overreach.''\par \par \uc2 The law, which bans abortions after fetal cardiac activity is detected, at about six weeks of pregnancy, has altered the landscape for abortions in the nation's second-most-populous state because of its unique structure, which bars state officials from enforcing its provisions, leaving that instead to private citizens.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration sued to halt the law, and on Wednesday, a U.S. District Court judge in Austin, Robert L. Pitman, granted the Justice Department's request to halt its enforcement. In his 113-page opinion, he wrote that because of the law, ''women have been unlawfully prevented from exercising control over their own lives in ways that are protected by the Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 In its brief ruling on Friday, the appeals court panel, made up of Judges Carl E. Stewart, Catharina Haynes and James C. Ho, called on lawyers for the Biden administration to respond to Texas' appeal by Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law went into effect at the start of September after the Supreme Court, in an emergency ruling, declined to intervene, and it immediately stopped a vast majority of abortions in Texas. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers feared the potential liability created by the law, which allows private citizens from anywhere in the United States to bring lawsuits against anyone who performs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or ''aids or abets'' the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Women in Texas seeking abortions after six weeks of pregnancy have since been forced to travel out of state for procedures, creating a surge in demand at clinics in bordering states like Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 While the ruling by the three-judge panel is only temporary, the Fifth Circuit is among the most conservative in the country and appeared likely to eventually side with Texas. Legal experts said that the decision on Friday evening sent a strong signal that the appeals court would not agree with the decision by Judge Pitman, who was appointed by President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''The Supreme Court needs to step in and stop this madness,'' \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , president and chief executive of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in a statement. ''It's unconscionable that the Fifth Circuit stayed such a well-reasoned decision that allowed constitutionally protected services to return in Texas.''\par \par \uc2 Two of the judges on the panel joined the appeals court during Republican presidential administrations: Judge Haynes was appointed by President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , and Judge Ho by President Donald J. Trump, while Judge Stewart was appointed by President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Judge Ho is also a former solicitor general of Texas, and he worked for Gov. Greg Abbott when Mr. Abbott was the state's attorney general.\par \par \uc2 ''This is an answered prayer,'' said Kimberlyn Schwartz, a spokeswoman for Texas Right to Life, which lobbied for the law. ''We are confident Texas will continue to defeat these attacks on our lifesaving efforts.''\par \par \uc2 Katie Benner contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Katie Benner contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 A women's rights rally at the Texas Capitol in Austin this month included support for \uc2  rights. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ILANA PANICH-LINSMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211010ehaa0004r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
