\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Texas's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Clinics Holding Off After Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sabrina Tavernise and Ruth Graham \par \uc2 1,292 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 At least six clinics had returned to performing the procedure, but most of the state's roughly two dozen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics were waiting, weighing the risks under the new law.\par \par \uc2 At least six clinics in Texas returned to performing abortions the day after a federal judge halted enforcement of the nation's most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure, but a majority had not, a reflection of the power of the law the judge froze.\par \par \uc2 The novel law, which banned most abortions in the state after cardiac activity is detected, about six weeks into pregnancy, continued to exert control over many clinics. Planned Parenthood, whose Texas affiliates operate seven centers that offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, said on Thursday that despite the ruling it was not carrying out abortions banned under the measure.\par \par \uc2 The law, which went into effect last month, has changed the landscape of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fight through a unique design that makes it particularly difficult to challenge in court by putting enforcement on citizens, not the state. On Wednesday night, Judge Robert L. Pitman of U.S. District Court in Austin sided with the Justice Department, which had sued to halt the law's enforcement.\par \par \uc2 But after weighing the risks, most of the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics -- there are about 24 across Texas -- decided not to resume abortions on women whose pregnancies exceeded six weeks because of another of the law's unique features: Clinics can be sued retroactively for up to four years for any abortions they provide while the measure is blocked.\par \par \uc2 ''Today we are not scheduling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointments past six weeks while we figure out what is possible,'' said Ianthe Metzger, a spokeswoman for Planned Parenthood Federation of America.\par \par \uc2 She said the situation was fluid.\par \par \uc2 ''The status quo on the ground hasn't changed today,'' she said on Thursday, ''but tomorrow it might,'' depending on affiliate clinics' conversations with their lawyers and staff members.\par \par \uc2 Kelly Krause, a spokeswoman for the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which represents clinics in court, said at least six Texas clinics were either providing abortions past six weeks on Thursday, or were scheduling appointments for women to soon have the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Four of them are operated by Whole Woman's Health. Amy Hagstrom Miller, the group's chief executive, said that late Wednesday night, employees started calling women who had previously signed consent forms and complied with Texas' 24-hour waiting period for abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''We were able to see a few people as early as 8, 9 this morning,'' she said on Thursday, noting that they had gotten abortions.\par \par \uc2 She also said that staff members at the clinics were scheduling abortions for Friday. She said doctors at other independent clinics in the state had also begun scheduling patients whose pregnancies were past six weeks for abortions, but refused to say how many or which clinics.\par \par \uc2 Still, clinics face an uphill battle. The state of Texas has already notified the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit, one of the most conservative courts in the country, that it would appeal. The suspension by Judge Pitman, who was appointed by President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  in 2014, could be overruled relatively soon, potentially within days, legal experts said.\par \par \uc2 For some clinics, that was too much of a risk.\par \par \uc2 ''We look forward to resuming \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care the moment we sense the relief in place is durable,'' said \uc2 Jeffrey Hons\uc2 , president and chief executive of Planned Parenthood South Texas.\par \par \uc2 The odds are good -- but not certain -- that the Fifth Circuit will freeze Judge Pitman's injunction, said Stephen Vladeck, a law professor at the University of Texas at Austin. But he said that would not be the end of the saga.\par \par \uc2 ''It's just the next act in the drama,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The national fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reached a critical point. The Supreme Court, in its new term that began this week, will soon consider a 15-week ban in Mississippi, the first full argument of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case to be heard by the court's new six-to-three conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 There is a chance the court could overturn Roe v. Wade, the landmark 1973 case that established \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a constitutional right. If it does, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would become illegal in at least 11 states where lawmakers have passed legislation that would go into effect if Roe falls, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I think it's fair to say that Roe v. Wade is in the most peril it has been in since 1973,'' said B. Jessie Hill, a law professor at Case Western Reserve University in Cleveland, who has represented \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in federal cases. ''It would be hard to overstate how much things are about to change.''\par \par \uc2 The Texas law is unique because it forbids the state to enforce it, and instead deputizes ordinary citizens to sue anyone who performs abortions or ''aids and abets'' them. They get $10,000 if they are successful. That feature makes it difficult to challenge in court because there is no single entity responsible for enforcement. The result has been a ban on nearly all abortions in the state since the law, called the Heartbeat Act, went into effect on Sept. 1.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in about 20 other states have either expressed interest in or started work on copycat bills, said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , a policy expert at Guttmacher.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists in Texas said they were confident that Judge Pitman's ruling would not immediately increase the number of abortions to the levels seen before the law. \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  List, a national anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization, said in a statement on Wednesday that the Texas measure had ''saved more than 4,700 babies.''\par \par \uc2 Chelsey Youman, the Texas state director for Human Coalition, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, said she felt ''hopeful and confident'' that the Fifth Circuit would rule in its favor.\par \par \uc2 John Seago, the legislative director for Texas Right to Life, the largest anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group in the state, said that his organization was closely watching what clinics in Texas were saying, but that it would wait to gather evidence and build a case it believed would be strong. And that would take time, he said.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not running to the courthouse,'' he said. Still, he added: ''We are not going to sit this out. The real question is the timing and when is the right moment to file lawsuits if we have evidence.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have another avenue under the law -- to sue in federal court. Claimants would have to be from a state other than Texas and the legal fight they were picking would have to be at least $75,000 in value, which in this case could mean at least eight abortions considered illegal under the law, a hurdle that Mr. Vladeck argued made this route improbable.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vladeck said there was a potential scenario in which this Texas case goes to the Supreme Court and is joined with the Mississippi case currently on its docket; if so, the court could issue dual decisions that stop short of formally overruling Roe while upholding Mississippi's 15-week ban and striking down Texas' six-week ban along with its novel procedural approach.\par \par \uc2 ''If you are Chief Justice Roberts, that's an elegant solution,'' he said, referring to \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . ''It's looking like an increasingly attractive way to split the difference.''\par \par \uc2 Edgar Sandoval contributed reporting from San Antonio.\par \par \uc2 Edgar Sandoval contributed reporting from San Antonio.\par \par \uc2  rights advocates rallied at the Texas Capitol in Austin on Saturday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Ilana Panich-Linsman for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211008eha800055\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
