\par\par \uc2 SIDEBAR\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Conservatives Look Abroad in  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Case\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 969 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court hears arguments this fall in a big \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case from Mississippi, it will consider dueling accounts of international practices.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Not so long ago, conservatives found it maddening when U.S. judges cited foreign law in their decisions interpreting the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court took account of international trends in a 2005 decision eliminating the juvenile death penalty, for instance, Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  wrote a furious dissent. ''The basic premise of the court's argument -- that American law should conform to the laws of the rest of the world -- ought to be rejected out of hand,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The justice also accused his colleagues of opportunism and hypocrisy. In other areas of the law, he wrote, the court had ignored conservative foreign decisions on criminal procedure, religion and, notably, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''To invoke alien law when it agrees with one's own thinking, and ignore it otherwise, is not reasoned decision-making, but sophistry,'' Justice Scalia wrote.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in Mississippi appeared to take a different view in 2018, when they enacted a law banning most abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 In the first legislative finding justifying the law, its drafters looked abroad for support. ''The United States is one of only seven nations in the world that permits nontherapeutic or elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -on-demand after the 20th week of gestation,'' the finding said. ''In fact, fully 75 percent of all nations do not permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 12 weeks' gestation, except (in most instances) to save the life and to preserve the physical health of the mother.''\par \par \uc2 The law was a calculated challenge to Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prohibited states from banning the procedure before fetal viability, or around 23 or 24 weeks. The Supreme Court will hear arguments in December in a challenge to the law.\par \par \uc2 The lawmakers' statements about foreign practices appear to be generally correct. A fact-checking column in The Washington Post in 2017 largely confirmed the first one: ''This statistic seemed dubious at first, because it seemed extreme for just seven countries out of 198 to allow elective abortions after 20 weeks of pregnancy,'' it said. ''But upon further digging, the data back up the claim.''\par \par \uc2 And 12 weeks is a common nominal limit, said Mary Ziegler, a law professor at Florida State University, though the social context is usually quite different.\par \par \uc2 ''Most places in the world have something like 12 weeks, but with public health insurance,'' she said. ''They pay for it. If you want to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first 12 weeks, there's no real reason that you can't.''\par \par \uc2 Martha F. Davis, a law professor at Northeastern University, added that the limits were usually subject to significant exceptions for patients who needed later abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''Many nations, but not all, and not our closest peers, have cutoffs that on paper are pre-viability,'' she said. ''But they make very many exceptions that allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  much more liberally.''\par \par \uc2 At his confirmation hearings in 2005, Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . questioned the use of foreign law in U.S. constitutional cases, saying it was bound to be selective.\par \par \uc2 ''In foreign law, you can find anything you want,'' he said. ''Looking at foreign law for support is like looking out over a crowd and picking out your friends.''\par \par \uc2 In a Supreme Court brief, officials in Mississippi focused on the nation's adversaries. ''The United States finds itself in the company of China and North Korea as some of the only countries that permit elective abortions after 20 weeks' gestation,'' the brief said.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers challenging the Mississippi law asked the court to consider other nations.\par \par \uc2 ''In countries with legal traditions and democratic values most comparable to the United States, such as Great Britain and Canada, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal until at least viability,'' they wrote. ''And many countries that have limits earlier in pregnancy continue to permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for broad social and health reasons after that point, functionally allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in pregnancy.''\par \par \uc2 Dueling friend-of-the-court briefs in the Mississippi case also supported Chief Justice Roberts's observation about selectivity.\par \par \uc2 In one brief, international law professors supporting the Mississippi law said that ''France, Italy, Germany, Spain, Norway and Switzerland have a gestational limit of 14 weeks or earlier for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand, allowing later exceptions only on restricted medical grounds.'' The brief cited data gathered by the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the other side, a brief from another set of international and comparative law scholars supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Mississippi focused on the countries that it said had similar legal traditions to the United States, notably Canada, New Zealand and Britain, which ''permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to or around viability.''\par \par \uc2 ''Beyond their broadly permissive laws,'' the brief said, ''these countries also support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and reproductive decision-making through universal health care, access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services and access to contraception.''\par \par \uc2 The brief added that recent international trends had been toward easier access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with more than 50 countries liberalizing their laws in the past 25 years. By contrast, the brief said, overruling Roe ''would put the United States in the company of countries like Poland and Nicaragua as one of only a few countries moving towards greater restrictions on legal access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past 20 years.''\par \par \uc2 Professor Ziegler said there was something artificial about the recent conservative attentiveness to foreign nations with roughly 12-week limits.\par \par \uc2 ''People who are anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are disingenuous about this, because they're not proposing 12 weeks,'' she said. ''They're proposing six weeks, or they're proposing fertilization.''\par \par \uc2 Backers and foes of \uc2  rights last week in Jackson, Miss. (PHOTOGRAPH BY RORY DOYLE/REUTERS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211005eha50003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
