\par\par \uc2 CHARLES M. BLOW\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 If Men Needed the Abortions ...\b0\par\par\uc2 By Charles M. Blow \par \uc2 918 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, three women of color -- sitting members of Congress -- testified before the House Committee on Oversight and Reform about abortions they had received, describing, in some cases, the stigma attached to them.\par \par \uc2 At the hearing, entitled ''A Dire State: Examining the Urgent Need to Protect and Expand \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rights and Access in the United States,'' they spoke in wrenching terms about the choices they had made.\par \par \uc2 Representative Cori Bush, a Democrat from Missouri, talked about having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at age 17 after surviving a rape, saying simply: ''I was raped, I became pregnant and I chose to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 And she spoke about how the counseling before the procedure belittled and diminished her, saying she remembered ''being told that if I moved forward with this pregnancy, my baby would be 'jacked up' because the fetus was already malnourished and underweight. Being told that if I had this baby, I would wind up on food stamps and welfare.''\par \par \uc2 Then, as The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reported, Representative \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , Democrat of Washington, told her story. She had gotten an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''when she was a young mother caring for a very sick child and struggling to recover from postpartum depression so severe that she considered suicide. Her doctor told her that carrying a second child to term would be extremely risky for both her and the baby.''\par \par \uc2 And Representative Barbara Lee, Democrat of California, spoke about getting a ''back alley'' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Mexico as a teenager before \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legal in the United States.\par \par \uc2 All of this testimony came at a time when Congress is debating codifying Roe v. Wade as means of protecting it from Republican assaults, but for me these testimonies were powerful in another way: They once again underscored just how difficult a decision this is for many women and the degree to which others feel empowered to intrude on the decisions they make.\par \par \uc2 This is particularly true of men, who have never been faced with the choice and never will. We need to hear these stories to understand it.\par \par \uc2 On one level, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  strikes to the core of people, because it makes us consider the question of when a fertilized egg becomes a person.\par \par \uc2 If you believe that it is from the moment of conception, there is nothing anyone can tell you that would make it OK to terminate a pregnancy, because to you the entire enterprise is one of killing babies.\par \par \uc2 But is a clump of cells a child? Is a fetus a child? These debates can quickly veer into the philosophical and religious.\par \par \uc2 Since 1973, Roe v. Wade has protected a woman's right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before a fetus becomes viable outside the womb, about 24 weeks into the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Now, that too is under attack.\par \par \uc2 I listened to the testimony of these women with great humility, from an outside place, with the privilege of knowing that my body was not built for this purpose. The ability to carry a life and bring it into world is a tremendous power, and a gift. But carrying a pregnancy to term is simply not right for many women at the point in which they become pregnant.\par \par \uc2 It is at that point that their bodies become a battleground. At what stage of pregnancy are they still the person in control and at what point must they submit to being a vessel for a ''person'' growing inside them? At what stage is choice eliminated?\par \par \uc2 Viability is the legal standard, whatever anyone may believe.\par \par \uc2 We also have to remember the shame that many women who have had abortions have expressed, even if they can now say that they are past shame. Why should any woman feel shame about a difficult choice? They have enough to deal with without the rest of society weighing in on their choices.\par \par \uc2 In the first months after conception, after a woman knows for sure that she is pregnant and before the fetus become viable, she needs to feel free to make choices about her body, her health and her future. That shouldn't be subject to community approval. That shouldn't be against the law.\par \par \uc2 The law passed in Texas banning most abortions after the first six weeks of a pregnancy, before many women even know that they are pregnant, is an outrage and an offense. It puts the state -- and even deputized citizen vigilantes -- between a woman and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 It feels like we are in a very dangerous place in this country, like Roe v. Wade is under threat in a way that it hasn't been in recent memory. It feels like we are on a precipice of pushing women back in time and back into alleys. It feels like we could once again be on the edge of criminalizing choice.\par \par \uc2 If men were the ones who got pregnant, this would never have happened. Men wouldn't stand for it. Women shouldn't either.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) , and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Representative Cori Bush, who spoke of getting an \uc2  after surviving a rape. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Whitney Curtis for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211004eha400031\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
