\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Judge Weighs Suspending Strict Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Benner and Sabrina Tavernise \par \uc2 900 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Justice Department said the law was intended to ''violate the Constitution,'' and asked for it to be suspended while the courts determine if it is legal.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- A federal judge heard arguments on Friday from the State of Texas and the federal government on whether a Texas law that bans nearly all abortions in the state should be suspended while the courts decide if it is legal.\par \par \uc2 At issue is a restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law that Texas enacted in September that uses a unique legal approach -- deputizing private citizens to enforce it, instead of the state. The law, also known as the Texas Heartbeat Act and Senate Bill 8, has had a chilling effect, with most of the state's roughly two dozen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics no longer offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in cases in which cardiac activity is detected, which usually begins at around six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department sued Texas last month over the law. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick B. Garland\uc2  called the enforcement mechanism ''an unprecedented'' effort to prevent women from exercising their constitutionally protected right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said that no matter their stand on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Americans should fear that the Texas law could become a model to restrict other constitutionally protected rights.\par \par \uc2 At the hearing on Friday, before Judge Robert L. Pitman, a Federal District Court judge in Austin, William T. Thompson, a lawyer for the State of Texas, asserted that the federal government had no grounds to be arguing the case, because the law did not harm it.\par \par \uc2 ''If the Texas Heartbeat Act actually, you know, created a liability for the federal government, there would be at least a potential injury there that would be worth litigating,'' Mr. Thompson said. ''But the Heartbeat Act doesn't do that.''\par \par \uc2 While Senate Bill 8 was passed by the Texas Legislature and signed into law by Gov. Greg Abbott, a Republican, it is to be enforced by private citizens filing civil lawsuits. Those private parties are entitled to $10,000 and their legal fees recovered if they successfully sue those who aid in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is restricted under the statute.\par \par \uc2 That makes the law in Texas unique from ones restricting access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have been passed in other states, all of which have been suspended while they work their way through the court system. Last month, the Supreme Court, largely on procedural grounds, rejected a request by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics to suspend the Texas law, but made clear that the law could still be challenged on constitutional grounds.\par \par \uc2 The state has argued in a court filing that civil suits brought by private citizens under the new law in Texas State courts were the ''proper cases for deciding the constitutionality of the challenged statute.'' Few such suits have been filed so far.\par \par \uc2 Brian Netter, a lawyer for the Justice Department, argued on Friday that, contrary to Texas' claims, Senate Bill 8 did directly harm the federal government because it violated the constitutional principal that federal law took precedence over state law if a conflict arose between the two.\par \par \uc2 ''This suit is necessary because S.B. 8 represents a thus far unprecedented attack on the supremacy of the federal government, and the federal Constitution,'' Mr. Netter told the court, calling the measure ''a truly extraordinary law designed to outflank the federal government.''\par \par \uc2 He said that the federal government also had to challenge the law because it effectively deprived a group of citizens a constitutional right. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained federal protection in 1973, in a landmark Supreme Court decision, Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Netter argued that the structure of the law amounted to incentivizing ''vigilante bounty hunters.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Thompson disputed that characterization. ''This is not some kind of vigilante scheme as opposing counsel suggests,'' he said, adding that the law ''uses the normal and lawful process of justice in Texas.''\par \par \uc2 Judge Pitman asked Mr. Thompson about the law's enforcement mechanism.\par \par \uc2 ''We're always directed to look for people with responsibility for enforcement, and so can you help me by identifying those people who you believe would be proper?'' he asked.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Thompson responded that the Texas Legislature ''expressly prohibited public officials at any level of Texas or local government from enforcing it. And so I don't think there is someone I can identify for the court because I don't think any such person exists.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Netter argued that the private citizens who would potentially sue were effectively state actors. ''This is not a case in which an individual is coming into court because something has happened to him or her,'' he said. ''The supposed wrong here is Texas' belief that abortions after six weeks are wrong.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Thompson pushed back against that argument.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't think that the private plaintiffs are put in the shoes of the state,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Netter suggested Texas was being disingenuous in its defense of the law.\par \par \uc2 ''The state's pitch is, this is all just ordinary, but I think we all know that's not the case,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Protests at the Texas Capitol against a new law that bans nearly all abortions in the state. The Supreme Court last month rejected a request to block the measure, largely on procedural grounds. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JAY JANNER/AUSTIN AMERICAN-STATESMAN, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020211002eha20003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
