\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Bill on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Approved by the House\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carl Hulse \par \uc2 839 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Women's Health Protection Act is unlikely to advance in the Senate, but its passage reflected a sense of urgency by Democrats to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The House narrowly approved a measure on Friday intended to enact \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into federal law, as Democrats sought to counter efforts at the state level to restrict abortions and growing hostility to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights from the conservative-leaning Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Acting after the high court refused to block stringent new Texas limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , lawmakers voted 218 to 211 to send the Women's Health Protection Act to the Senate, where it faces a Republican filibuster and is unlikely to pass.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , Democrat of New York and the majority leader, said in a statement on Friday that he would schedule a vote on the measure, underscoring that Democrats see the struggle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a winning political issue even if the bill will not reach President Biden's desk. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are already emerging as a point of contention in some midterm election campaigns.\par \par \uc2 ''We are currently seeing unprecedented and unconscionable Republican attacks on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the county laced with vicious vigilantism,'' Mr. Schumer said in a joint statement with three Democratic leaders in the Senate: \uc2 Richard J. Durbin\uc2  of Illinois, \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  of Washington and Richard Blumenthal of Connecticut, the bill's author in that chamber. ''Congress must assert its role to protect the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Democratic authors of the measure heralded the House vote as historic, given what they described as mounting threats to Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They framed it as a sharp rebuke to the Supreme Court for allowing the Texas law prohibiting most abortions after six weeks to go into effect earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 ''If the justices over there in that building won't act, this House of Representatives will act,'' said Representative \uc2 Diana DeGette\uc2 , a Colorado Democrat and a longtime backer of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , who called for the measure to be considered soon after the Texas decision, lashed out at the court for failing to intercede to block the new law, arguing that it had ignored 50 years of precedent on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''When this court embraced this shameful Texas law, they brought shame to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 ,'' she said before the vote. ''What were they thinking, or were they thinking, or were they were just rubber-stamping what they were sent to the court to do?''\par \par \uc2 Republicans uniformly opposed the measure, which they called extreme and said would lead to abortions at all stages of pregnancy and overturn hundreds of state laws. They said the measure went far beyond codifying Roe and should more rightfully be called the ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  On Demand Until Birth Act.''\par \par \uc2 ''This \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -on-demand bill would destroy our country's future,'' said Representative \uc2 Jackie Walorski\uc2 , Republican of Indiana. ''A child in the womb is a living person, and yet my colleagues on the other side remain obsessed with killing unborn babies in the name of female empowerment.''\par \par \uc2 ''This will nullify every modest pro-life restriction ever enacted by the states,'' said Representative Christopher H. Smith, Republican of New Jersey. ''This bill constitutes an existential threat to unborn babies.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats said the measure was urgently needed since the Supreme Court was set to consider a Mississippi law later this year that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists fear could lead to further erosion of Roe v. Wade, or its outright overturning.\par \par \uc2 In the 50-50 Senate, Mr. Schumer has been reluctant to force votes on measures that lack support from at least a bare majority, since doing so would spotlight party divisions.\par \par \uc2 At least two Democrats have not endorsed the Women's Health Protection Act, and even Republicans who have supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the past have said it goes well beyond the reach of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But holding votes on the measure is a way for Democrats to demonstrate that they consider \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a priority and force Republicans to go on the record against legislation that Democrats see as popular, particularly with women voters who believed that the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access had been settled with the Supreme Court's 1973 ruling.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters hailed the House action and urged the Senate to take up the measure.\par \par \uc2 ''The historic House passage of the Women's Health Protection Act is an important step in protecting the right to access an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the U.S., and halting the wave of harmful and deeply unpopular \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions across the country,'' said \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , the president of the Planned Parenthood Federation of America. ''Now the Senate must follow suit and immediately pass this critical legislation.''\par \par \uc2 An \uc2  rights bill is unlikely to be adopted, but its passage shows a sense of urgency by Democrats to maintain \uc2  access. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210925eh9p0003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
