\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Providers Ask Court to Revisit Case\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,018 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The new filing asks the justices to use an unusual procedure to rule on a law that bans most abortions after six weeks and was designed to avoid judicial scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas returned to the Supreme Court on Thursday, asking the justices to take another look at their challenge to a state law that bans most abortions after six weeks and was designed to evade review in federal court.\par \par \uc2 By a 5-to-4 vote on Sept. 1, the court refused to block the law, citing the ''complex and novel'' procedural questions it presented. Since then, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas have turned away most patients seeking the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In the new filing, the providers asked the court to grant immediate review of the core issue in the case, leapfrogging an appeals court. That procedure, called ''certiorari before judgment,'' is rarely used, typically in cases involving national crises like President \uc2 Harry S. Truman\uc2 's seizure of the steel industry and President Richard M. Nixon's refusal to turn over tape recordings to a special prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 The providers said the court should use the procedure to decide what they said was a pressing question: ''whether a state can insulate from federal-court review a law that prohibits the exercise of a constitutional right by delegating to the general public the authority to enforce that prohi\u173\'00\'ADbition through civil actions.''\par \par \uc2 The appeals court, the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit, in New Orleans, has not yet issued a decision in an appeal pending before it. But ''the writing is on the wall,'' the providers told the justices. ''And although the Fifth Circuit expedited the appeal, it will not hold argument until December at the earliest.''\par \par \uc2 ''Meanwhile, Texans are in crisis,'' the providers wrote.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, known as S.B. 8, has novel features. The law, which makes no exceptions for pregnancies resulting from incest or rape, bars state officials from enforcing it and instead deputizes private individuals to sue anyone who performs the procedure or ''aids and abets'' it.\par \par \uc2 That makes it hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to know whom to sue, as lawsuits seeking to block laws as unconstitutional typically name the officials charged with enforcing them as defendants. When the providers filed suit in federal court, they named, among others, every state trial court judge and county court clerk in Texas.\par \par \uc2 While it may be hard to sue to challenge the law, it allows private citizens to file suits against doctors, staff members at clinics, counselors, people who help pay for the procedure, and even an Uber driver taking a patient to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. Plaintiffs, who do not need to live in Texas, have any connection to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or show any injury from it, are entitled to $10,000 and their legal fees recovered if they win. Prevailing defendants are not entitled to legal fees.\par \par \uc2 ''Faced with the threat of unlimited lawsuits from the general popu\u173\'00\'ADlace and the prospect of ruinous liability if they violate the ban, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have been forced to com\u173\'00\'ADply,'' the providers wrote. ''As a result, Texans with means must now travel hundreds of miles each way to other states during a pandemic, just to exercise a clearly established federal right. The surge of Texans seeking out-of-state ap\u173\'00\'ADpointments for this time-sensitive medical care is causing backlogs in those states, delaying abortions by weeks for Texans and non-Texans alike.''\par \par \uc2 The majority in the unsigned order issued on Sept. 1 suggested that one procedurally proper way for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to challenge the law was to get sued and raise the unconstitutionality of the law as a defense. A few suits against a doctor who said he provided an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been filed.\par \par \uc2 In their Supreme Court filing, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers said that procedural route was inadequate.\par \par \uc2 ''The few cases pending in state court could take months, if not years, to wend through the state-court system before they could provide statewide relief,'' they wrote. ''And if someone sued under S.B. 8 prevails and the claimant chooses not to appeal, peti\u173\'00\'ADtioners will have no opportunity to receive a statewide ruling.''\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . seemed to invite a fuller challenge to the Texas law when he dissented from the court's Sept. 1 order.\par \par \uc2 ''Although the court denies the applicants' request for emergency relief today, the court's order is emphatic in making clear that it cannot be understood as sustaining the constitutionality of the law at issue,'' he wrote. ''But although the court does not address the constitutionality of this law, it can of course promptly do so when that question is properly presented. At such time the question could be decided after full briefing and oral argument.''\par \par \uc2 That is basically what the providers asked for on Thursday. The Supreme Court is likely to order a prompt response to the request from state officials. If it grants review, it would presumably hear arguments in the case in the coming months, perhaps alongside a separate case challenging Mississippi's 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that is scheduled to be heard on Dec. 1. The officials in that case have asked the justices to overrule Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision establishing a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under the Roe decision and later precedents, states may not ban abortions before fetal viability, or about 23 weeks. Both the Texas and Mississippi laws are unconstitutional under those precedents.\par \par \uc2 ''The gravity of the circumstances and the para\u173\'00\'ADmount importance of the question presented warrant this court's intervention,'' the providers told the justices. ''The court should act now to resolve the question pre\u173\'00\'ADsented on an expedited basis, with the benefit of briefing and argument that was impossible when pe\u173\'00\'ADtitioners filed their emergency application.''\par \par \uc2 A rally outside the Supreme Court on Sept. 4, days after justices refused to block a Texas law that bans most abortions after six weeks. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210924eh9o0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
