\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 With Roe Under Threat, House Plans to Vote on Bill to Counter  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Curbs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carl Hulse \par \uc2 1,168 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The measure has little chance of becoming law, but its consideration reflects a view among Democrats that the issue could resonate in midterm congressional elections.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- House Democrats plan on Friday to push through broad legislation to uphold \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, taking urgent action after a major Supreme Court setback as they brace for a ruling next year that could further roll back access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The House vote will be largely symbolic given that the bill, the Women's Health Protection Act, has little chance of advancing because of Republican opposition in the Senate. But House Democrats' decision to consider it reflects their view that the issue could resonate strongly in the midterm elections next year, particularly if female voters see the Supreme Court action as a threat to rights that many believed had been long settled.\par \par \uc2 Democrats moved swiftly to schedule action on the measure after the court refused this month to block a Texas law that prohibits most abortions after six weeks of gestation. It would guarantee the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through federal law, pre-empting hundreds of state laws governing the procedure around the country. Democrats argue that it would codify Roe v. Wade, the 1973 Supreme Court decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bill's authors say they began drafting it a decade ago in response to emerging efforts at the state level to impose stringent requirements on those seeking and providing abortions, as well as the increasingly conservative makeup of the court. They say that the court's current membership and its hostility toward \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have validated the approach, and that time is of the essence because the justices are set to rule next year on a Mississippi law that severely restricts abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''It became very evident that we needed to have something that would push back against all these state restrictions,'' said Representative Judy Chu, Democrat of California and the lead author of the measure. ''We could see that change was possible at the Supreme Court, and we knew we had to make sure that Roe v. Wade was protected.''\par \par \uc2 But opponents of the law -- including some Republicans who have supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- argue that it would go far beyond the landmark court precedent, stripping states of much of their ability to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and impose measures intended to make the procedure safe. They say it would lead to many more abortions in the late stages of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''This legislation is really about a mandate by the federal government that would demand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand, without any consideration for anyone, including the conscience of the provider,'' said Representative Cathy McMorris Rodgers, Republican of Washington and a chief foe of the bill.\par \par \uc2 Democratic backers of the measure say they are confident they have the support to win approval in the House, which has not previously voted on it. But the Democratic-led Senate might not take up the bill, which appears to be just short of majority support in that chamber.\par \par \uc2 At least two Democrats who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Senators Bob Casey of Pennsylvania and \uc2 Joe Manchin III\uc2  of West Virginia, are against the legislation. Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  of New York, the majority leader, has been reluctant to bring up measures in the evenly divided chamber that do not have at least 50 votes. Even if the bill could win a slim majority, Republicans would be certain to filibuster it, preventing it from advancing unless it could attract 60 votes, a number that seems far out of reach.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine, a Republican who backs \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and was seen as a potential vote for the new bill, said she would not support it in its current form.\par \par \uc2 ''I support codifying Roe,'' Ms. Collins said. ''Unfortunately, the bill that the House has drafted goes way beyond that.'' She argued that it would ''severely weaken'' protections afforded to health care providers who refused to perform abortions on religious or moral grounds.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration, pointing to the new Texas legislation, supports the bill.\par \par \uc2 ''In the wake of Texas' unprecedented attack, it has never been more important to codify this constitutional right and to strengthen health care access for all women, regardless of where they live,'' White House officials said in a policy statement. ''Our daughters and granddaughters deserve the same rights that their mothers and grandmothers fought for and won -- and that a clear majority of the American people support.''\par \par \uc2 The Democrats' strong push for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measure reflects a changing political dynamic in the party. In the past, Democratic leaders were reluctant to emphasize measures such as the women's health bill for fear of putting centrists in swing districts in a tough position and potentially alienating voters.\par \par \uc2 But as the ranks of centrist Democrats have shrunk, so have the numbers of lawmakers in the party who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Ms. Chu said she had found that her colleagues from competitive districts had been eager to sign onto the measure.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a pro-choice nation,'' Representative Jan Schakowsky, Democrat of Illinois and a chief backer of the legislation, told the \uc2 House Rules Committee\uc2  this week as she urged the panel to send the bill to the floor. ''This is the majority view across most of the electorate.''\par \par \uc2 Multiple public opinion polls conducted since the Supreme Court agreed to take on the Mississippi case have reflected strong support for keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal -- nearly 60 percent in some surveys -- though that support typically declines for abortions performed later in pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Opponents say even those who may back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to some degree still want the procedure closely regulated and states to establish safeguards and limits.\par \par \uc2 ''Our biggest issue is definitely that this takes away the ability of state lawmakers and local lawmakers to solve problems that they have identified and that their constituents raise,'' said Katie Glenn, the government affairs counsel of \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2 . ''Thousands of state laws are at risk from this bill.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Chu said it was some of those very laws that needed to be struck down, because their true intent was to make it more difficult to have abortions and to discourage women from seeking them. Although the measure was unlikely to clear the Senate, she said it was necessary that Democrats act given the Texas law and the probability of a major Supreme Court ruling after arguments set for December over Mississippi's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''It is important for us to make a strong statement about what is possible in Congress,'' Ms. Chu said, ''to protect women's freedom to make a choice.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats moved swiftly to schedule action on the Women's Health Protection Act after the Supreme Court refused this month to block a Texas law that prohibits most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy.  (PHOTOGRAPH BY J. Scott Applewhite/Associated Press FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210924eh9o0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
