\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The Fight for  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights in Mexico Isn't Over\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melissa Ayala \par \uc2 1,099 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 27\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Growing up in the 1990s in a Catholic household outside of Mexico City, I learned as a young girl that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was out of the question. When I reached my adolescent years, the only representation I had ever seen of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in pop culture was in the film ''The Crime of Padre Amaro.'' In the movie, the priest impregnates a young woman, then takes her to an unsanitary and illegal clinic. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  goes wrong; she dies. The message landed: I was sure that having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would lead to death.\par \par \uc2 This month Mexico got a new message. On Sept. 7 the justices of the Mexican Supreme Court unanimously ruled that it is unconstitutional to criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The court then stated, unequivocally, that our Constitution guarantees the right to choose. No Mexican women or people with the ability to become pregnant should be prosecuted for exercising their rights.\par \par \uc2 Even as feminists across the country celebrate this decision, we also must recognize where credit is due. For more than 29 years, feminists have organized themselves in nonprofit groups such as GIRE (where I work), Fondo Mar\u237\'00\'EDa and Balance. Women have taken to the streets again and again across Latin America demanding that our governments guarantee our rights.\par \par \uc2 Grass-roots movements have transformed the narrative and have made more and more Mexicans see that we need sexual education to discover our bodies, contraceptives to enjoy our sexuality and legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to own our own decisions. (The motto in Spanish is ''Educaci\u243\'00\'F3n sexual para descubrir, anticonceptivos para disfrutar, aborto legal para decidir.'') The feminist movement has insisted for years that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  involves and affects all women, no matter their social status.\par \par \uc2 When I left my home state, Puebla, in 2010 to attend law school in Mexico City, I learned that first-trimester abortions were made legal in the capital of our country in 2007. From my feminist law professors, my fellow students and I learned, often for the first time, about the right to choose, a right our Constitution grants.\par \par \uc2 After Mexico City opened the door to legalization, states slowly followed. First came Oaxaca in 2019, and this year Hidalgo and Veracruz joined what feminists call the Marea Verde, or the Green Tide. But Mexico is a federation, made up of states. That means your rights depend on where you are standing, a legal system very similar to that of the United States.\par \par \uc2 This month the Mexican Supreme Court offered hope to all women and girls in our country. The justices said what has long been intuitive to feminist activists: that someone who is not yet born does not have the same protection as someone who already is alive.\par \par \uc2 The court stated that women and nonbinary people must not be prosecuted criminally for having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, the decision does not translate into an immediate decriminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all states, since \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still a crime on the books in 28 local criminal codes. The decision means that no judge may send to prison or sanction women or nonbinary persons who exercise their right to choose to terminate a pregnancy. In other words, technically, a woman could still be taken before a judge and exposed before the community, though she would not see jail time.\par \par \uc2 We are early yet in the story of our rights in Mexico. Not being sent to jail does not mean \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is accessible for everyone. Women living outside of Mexico City, Oaxaca, Veracruz or Hidalgo -- the areas where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have progressed the farthest -- still have to travel to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That restriction disproportionately affects those who are economically vulnerable. And in Mexico, that is an enormous restriction. In my country, we say poverty has a female face; out of 65.5 million women, 50 million are in poverty or at risk of economic or social hardship.\par \par \uc2 Feminist activists should continue to demand that their local representatives work to reform the criminal codes to eliminate the articles that consider voluntary \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime. This is not only legally relevant; it is imperative in order to start eradicating the social stigma that still surrounds those who decide to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Mexico, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long been cast in the context of shame -- just as I first learned from Padre Amaro. This is finally starting to change: Protests by those who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were held outside the doors of the Supreme Court. But the prayer and protest did not seem to have any effect on the arguments of the justices.\par \par \uc2 Now it is essential that the Mexican media and pop culture portray \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as what it is: a right and a choice. I write this thinking of all the girls who, like me, grew up equating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with death or jail. Thanks to the Mexican justices, this idea may start to disappear. We still have to continue fighting to guarantee that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a safe, legal, accessible and free medical procedure.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, the feminist performance art collective Las Tesis created a feminist anthem denouncing violence against women that has become a theme song in the fight to legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The chant quickly spread through Latin America. In the song, ''\uc2 Un\uc2  violador en tu camino'' (''A Rapist in Your Path''), Las Tesis berated those who judge us for being born women. The group first performed this anthem in Santiago in 2019 and it was soon sung around the world, including by hundreds of thousands in Mexico City. Days before the Supreme Court made its ruling, Mexican women across the country quoted this song on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Mexican Supreme Court and our justices have now sent a signal to the entire Latin American region, where women continue to face obstacles to have a safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . From Argentina to Mexico, the green tide continues the fight for our rights.\par \par \uc2 Melissa Ayala is a lawyer specializing in constitutional law and feminist legal theory. She is the litigation coordinator of GIRE, a feminist organization dedicated to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and reproductive freedom based in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Women in Mexico City chanting in support of legal and safe \uc2 , February 2020. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Edgard Garrido/Reuters FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210923eh9n0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
