\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 First Citizen Cases on Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law May Settle Constitutionality\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ruth Graham, Adam Liptak and J. David Goodman \par \uc2 1,566 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Legal experts said two lawsuits filed this week might test the constitutionality of the Texas law more than federal challenges by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 DALLAS -- When the United States' most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law went into effect in Texas on Sept. 1, it worked exactly as intended: It effectively stopped all abortions in the second-most populous state.\par \par \uc2 But its very ingenuity -- that ordinary citizens, and not state officials, enforce it -- has begun to unleash lawsuits that are out of the control of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement that fought for the law.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, a man in Arkansas and another in Illinois, both disbarred lawyers with no apparent association with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, filed separate suits against a San Antonio doctor who publicly wrote about performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The suits appear to be the first legal actions taken under the law, known as Senate Bill 8, which deputizes private citizens, no matter where they live, to sue doctors or anyone else who ''aids and abets'' an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  performed after a fetus's cardiac activity is detected.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts said the lawsuits filed in state court might be the most likely way to definitively resolve the constitutionality of the Texas law, which has withstood legal tests. Two more sweeping challenges filed in federal court, brought by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and the Justice Department, raise difficult procedural questions.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders in Texas said they never expected many people to actually file lawsuits, thinking the process would be too costly and onerous.\par \par \uc2 ''These out-of-state suits are not what the bill is intended for,'' said Chelsey Youman, the Texas state director and national legislative adviser for Human Coalition, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group that said it had no plans to file a lawsuit against the physician, Dr. Alan Braid, or to encourage others to do so.\par \par \uc2 ''The goal is to save as many lives as possible, and the law is working,'' Ms. Youman said, adding that the notion behind the law was that the mere threat of liability would be so intimidating that providers would simply comply.\par \par \uc2 Early on, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists warned that the law would lead to a Wild West, in which vigilantes would sue anyone associated with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from ride-share drivers to relatives of pregnant women, in order to score a payout. The law's unique enforcement mechanism, designed to circumvent judicial review, invites private individuals to sue anyone involved with the procedure other than the pregnant woman. Should plaintiffs win, they would receive $10,000 and have their legal fees covered.\par \par \uc2 All was quiet until Saturday, when Dr. Braid wrote in The Washington Post that he had performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 6 to a woman who was ''beyond the state's new limit.'' He knew he was inviting lawsuits, he wrote, and ''taking a personal risk, but it's something I believe in strongly.''\par \par \uc2 Marc Hearron, senior counsel for the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group that represents Dr. Braid, said the doctor performed an ultrasound that detected cardiac activity before performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meaning the procedure did in fact violate the new state law.\par \par \uc2 The two lawsuits allow Dr. Braid, and those representing him, to assert the argument that the law is unconstitutional under both Roe v. Wade, which granted women the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Planned Parenthood v. Casey, which upheld it. If that defense is accepted on appeal, legal experts said, the cases could yield precedents effectively invalidating the Texas law -- a significant loss for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 From the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement's perspective, neither of the two men who filed suits this week is an ideal plaintiff. The Arkansas man, Oscar Stilley, who described himself in his suit as a ''disbarred and disgraced'' lawyer, said he was ''not pro-life'' and merely wanted to ''vindicate'' the law. The Illinois man, Felipe N. Gomez, described himself in his complaint as a ''pro-choice plaintiff.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Youman speculated that the lawsuits were ''plants,'' and she portrayed Dr. Braid's opinion essay as an attempt to bait a frivolous suit that would challenge the law's constitutionality in court.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gomez, 61, said in an interview that he had decided to file suit to challenge what he saw as government intrusion into private health care decisions. He described himself as ''pro-choice'' on a range of medical issues and wondered why some proponents of Texas' new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions supported mandates for pregnant women while also objecting to government mandates for vaccines to fight the coronavirus pandemic. (Mr. Gomez said he personally harbored a lifelong dislike of needles and did not support vaccine mandates.)\par \par \uc2 He also said he filed the suit as part of what he called a ''public interest law hobby'' that he hoped to pursue in his coming retirement. Mr. Gomez was suspended from practicing law in Illinois because of emails he had sent to other lawyers; he is currently in litigation over the matter, he said.\par \par \uc2 Until now, the Wild West scene that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists had warned of has not materialized. After the law's passage -- which the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement hailed as a clear-cut triumph -- clinics across the state immediately said they would comply; some reported that they had temporarily stopped providing any abortions at all.\par \par \uc2 That appeared to be enough for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, including Texas Right to Life, said John Seago, the group's legislative director. In the three weeks since the law went into effect, ''we don't have any evidence that a violation has occurred,'' he said, adding that the group estimated that ''over 2,000 lives have been saved so far by the Texas Heartbeat Act.''\par \par \uc2 The group, which lobbied for passage of the new law, set up a whistle-blower site for people to submit anonymous tips on illegal abortions. It received a flood of fake tips, and the group was working on ''extra security'' after switching servers before it would put the site back up, Mr. Seago said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Seago said the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement in the state was united in its wariness of Mr. Braid's vague but high-profile announcement of his violation. ''Notice he doesn't say, 'I'm open for business, anyone who wants an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , here's my address, schedule a visit,''' he said. ''It's a lot more calculated than that.''\par \par \uc2 The lawsuits were an entirely predictable result of S.B. 8's construction, said Mr. Hearron of the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because the law allows anyone to ''insert themselves into a health care decision between a patient and her doctor.''\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  turned down a request by a group of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, including the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to block the law in an unsigned order on Sept. 1, citing ''complex and novel'' procedural issues, the five justices in the majority wrote that their ruling was ''not based on any conclusion about the constitutionality of Texas' law, and in no way limits other procedurally proper challenges to the Texas law, including in Texas state courts.''\par \par \uc2 The proper way for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to challenge the law, the majority suggested, was to get sued and raise the unconstitutionality of the law as a defense. Now that it has been set in motion, a ruling from a state trial judge in one of the new cases could be appealed through the state court system in Texas and eventually reach the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''That is the nicest, cleanest way for it to get there,'' \uc2 Paul M. Smith\uc2 , a law professor at Georgetown, said at a briefing presented by its Supreme Court Institute on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is also scheduled to hear arguments on Dec. 1 in a challenge to Mississippi's ban on most abortions after 15 weeks. In that case, it has been asked to overrule Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision establishing a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If it does, the constitutional objection to the Texas law would presumably be rendered moot.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Gov. Greg Abbott of Texas signed a separate bill that outlaws providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drugs after seven weeks of pregnancy. That bill is set to go into effect in December.\par \par \uc2 Outside Alamo Women's Reproductive Services, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in San Antonio where Dr. Braid works, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist, Alejandra Gonzalez, paced in hopes of intercepting women on their way inside. ''We are praying for you. God bless you!'' Ms. Gonzalez, 18, shouted as a woman walked toward her car.\par \par \uc2 The new law, she said, has injected hope into the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. ''Our goal is to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as we know it,'' she said. ''That's what we are praying for.''\par \par \uc2 Ruth Graham reported from Dallas, Adam Liptak from Washington and J. David Goodman from Houston. Edgar Sandoval contributed reporting from San Antonio, and Michael S. Schmidt from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Graham reported from Dallas, Adam Liptak from Washington and J. David Goodman from Houston. Edgar Sandoval contributed reporting from San Antonio, and Michael S. Schmidt from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Protesters at the Supreme Court this month. The court turned down a request to block the law in an unsigned order on Sept. 1. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210922eh9m0004v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
