\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 In Spain, Abortions Are Legal, but Many Doctors Refuse to Perform Them\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Casey \par \uc2 1,478 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Many physicians in the country call themselves ''conscientious objectors'' and deny the procedures, often forcing women to travel long distances for one.\par \par \uc2 ZARAGOZA, Spain -- Dr. Mercedes Sobreviela, a gynecologist in this city in northeast Spain, believes it is a woman's choice whether she has an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She says the ''right decision'' for a woman is ''always the one she wants.''\par \par \uc2 But as a physician in Spain, Dr. Sobreviela believes she has the right to choose as well, and she has chosen not to perform abortions.\par \par \uc2 Her public hospital, University Clinic Hospital of Zaragoza, does not perform them either. In fact, no public hospital in the surrounding region of Arag\u243\'00\'F3n, which includes 1.3 million people, will do the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''We are doctors, our calling is as physicians, and we are here to help people live, not to decide this one lives and this one dies,'' Dr. Sobreviela said.\par \par \uc2 Spain liberalized its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in 2010. In the years before, it allowed women to get abortions in only extraordinary circumstances, but the new laws allow all women to get the procedure in the first 14 weeks of a pregnancy, without restrictions.\par \par \uc2 But the map of where abortions are available is drawn up less by national law than by Spain's doctors. In large numbers and across the country, doctors refuse to perform them.\par \par \uc2 The situation in Spain offers a window into what may await other countries at a time when sharply different measures in Texas and Mexico have revived the debate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Conservative lawmakers in Texas have all but banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state, while across the border, the Supreme Court in Mexico this month ruled to decriminalize abortions there.\par \par \uc2 The uncertainty in Mexico is whether doctors will provide the service -- a question that has already been answered by many doctors in Spain.\par \par \uc2 They call themselves ''conscientious objectors,'' a term coined by pacifists who refused military service. And like those who claimed a moral duty not to go to war, many doctors in Spain say performing abortions would violate their oath to do no harm -- a pledge, they say, that extends to the fetus.\par \par \uc2 ''It's one thing if you think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is right or wrong; each person will have their own criteria,'' says Dr. Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Jes\u250\'00\'FAs Barco, another gynecologist from Zaragoza who is an objector. ''It's another thing if I have to do it. That's different.''\par \par \uc2 Conscientious objection has gained ground in other countries, like Italy, where it was cited by doctors working in hospitals that largely do not perform abortions. And in Argentina, it has limited attempts to liberalize an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law passed there last year.\par \par \uc2 In five of the 17 autonomous regions in Spain -- the equivalent of states -- no public hospital offers abortions, according to the most recent government statistics. Women may still receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a subsidized private clinic, but in many cases, they must travel across state lines to obtain one.\par \par \uc2 That was what Erika Espinosa, 34, had to do in 2015 when her gynecologist in the city of Logro\u241\'00\'F1o would not perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after she asked for one.\par \par \uc2 ''The doctors try to convince you that you don't love your child for wanting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Ms. Espinosa, who went to the neighboring Navarra region to end her pregnancy. ''It felt like I was doing something clandestine.''\par \par \uc2 No official statistics exist on how many objecting doctors work in Spain. But the country's left-wing coalition government is concerned enough that in July, \uc2 Irene Montero\uc2 , the minister of equality, proposed changing the current \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law to place limits on the ability of doctors to become objectors.\par \par \uc2 ''Conscientious objection can't be incompatible with the rights of women, nor can it be an obstacle to them exercising their right to voluntarily terminate a pregnancy,'' the minister said in a written statement.\par \par \uc2 Such words have been met with sharp criticism from sectors of the Spanish medical community.\par \par \uc2 Eva Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Mart\u237\'00\'EDn, a pharmacist who heads the National Association for the Defense of Conscientious Objectors, a group that defends objecting doctors, called the proposals unfair and accused the government of ''radical feminism.''\par \par \uc2 ''It's part of a careless gender ideology that puts the freedom of women first, and leaves those of men in the gutter,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mart\u237\'00\'EDn said it was the doctors' duty to oppose any law that pushes them to take actions they see as unjust.\par \par \uc2 ''When there is a grave conflict between your conscience and the law, morally, in your interior, you must reject it,'' she said, adding that she had nine children as evidence of her anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views.\par \par \uc2 Some doctors have pushed to offer abortions at public hospitals. But they say it has rarely proved easy, not only because of objecting physicians, but also because doctors are rarely trained in the procedures during medical school.\par \par \uc2 When Dr. Abel Renuncio arrived at the Santiago Ap\u243\'00\'F3stol Hospital, a facility in the rural town of Miranda de Ebro, his team decided, for the first time, to offer abortions. Because the members of his team had not been trained to do them, they taught themselves using \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  protocols.\par \par \uc2 ''The technique is quite simple,'' said Dr. Renuncio, a gynecologist. ''We had no previous experience, but with willingness, it can be done.''\par \par \uc2 Yet a willingness to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  options may be the exception.\par \par \uc2 Silvia De Zordo, an Italian researcher with the Europe \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Project, which studies barriers to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said many older physicians who defend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights developed their views after witnessing the consequences of clandestine procedures and then participating in the legalization debate. But many of those doctors have since retired.\par \par \uc2 ''The new generations didn't have this experience or memory at all,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Among the provinces where abortions are no longer performed is Ja\u233\'00\'E9n, an olive-growing region in Spain's southern Andaluc\u237\'00\'EDa.\par \par \uc2 Juana Perag\u243\'00\'F3n, a feminist activist there, said that for a time, one clinic did offer them, though it was not subsidized by the state and charged women roughly $400 for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 But that clinic has been closed for remodeling for years, Ms. Perag\u243\'00\'F3n said. Many women are now sent as far as Seville to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , about 150 miles away.\par \par \uc2 ''We can see a concrete test of the distance between what the law says and how it's applied,'' Ms. Perag\u243\'00\'F3n said, saying a broad part of Spain is socially conservative and remains at odds with the law. ''It's impossible to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ja\u233\'00\'E9n.''\par \par \uc2 Spanish physicians like Dr. Sobreviela, the objector in Zaragoza, said the debate was not as clear-cut as some activists had framed it.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law passed in 2010 was in some ways ahead of where Spanish society was at the time, she said, and it caught many physicians off guard.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Sobreviela said she remembered attending a hospitalwide meeting in Zaragoza to discuss the new law, and doctors and others were asked to raise their hands if they objected. ''Ninety-nine percent of us were conscientious objectors,'' she said. ''Nearly everyone: the doctors, the nursing staff, the assistants, the guards.''\par \par \uc2 In her daily work, Dr. Sobreviela continues to focus on prenatal care, doing diagnostics on pregnant women to screen for signs of birth defects like Down syndrome or for heart problems that can be detected in fetuses.\par \par \uc2 At times, she said, most often when the defects may be fatal, a mother will ask her about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dr. Sobreviela said those could be very difficult conversations.\par \par \uc2 But she also offers warnings to those who choose to end their pregnancies. Under Spanish law, she said, doctors can explain possible ''psychological and social'' consequences of terminating a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The day before, a patient came to her after her fetus was diagnosed with heart problems, Dr. Sobreviela said.\par \par \uc2 ''She was in distress, and I was with her afterward, and she asked: 'This will happen soon, right? I want this problem to go away,''' Dr. Sobreviela recalled, saying the woman was going ahead with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''And I said: 'They're not going to get rid of your problem, they will just get rid of your pregnancy,'' she said. '''The problem comes when the pregnancy is gone and you have to sit with yourself, with your own conscience. Just you and your conscience.'''\par \par \uc2 Roser Toll Pifarr\u233\'00\'E9 contributed reporting from Barcelona.\par \par \uc2 Roser Toll Pifarr\u233\'00\'E9 contributed reporting from Barcelona.\par \par \uc2 ''It's one thing if you think \uc2  is right or wrong; each person will have their own criteria,'' said Dr. Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Jes\u250\'00\'FAs Barco, a gynecologist from Zaragoza who is an objector. ''It's another thing if I have to do it. That's different.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY Ana Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Ar\u233\'00\'E9valo Gosen for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210921eh9l0005g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
