\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Texas Doctor Who Performed An  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Faces 2 Lawsuits\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. David Goodman \par \uc2 883 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The suits, brought by men in Arkansas and Illinois, appear to be the first under a state law that bans most abortions after the six-week mark.\par \par \uc2 HOUSTON -- A man in Arkansas and another in Illinois on Monday filed what appeared to be the first legal actions under a strict new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in Texas that is enforced by ordinary citizens, regardless of where they live.\par \par \uc2 The Arkansas man, Oscar Stilley, who was described in the complaint as a ''disbarred and disgraced'' lawyer, said in an interview that he had filed the lawsuit against a Texas doctor, who publicly wrote about performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to test the provisions of the law. The Supreme Court declined to stop the law, which has effectively ended most abortions in the state since going into effect this month.\par \par \uc2 The law bars enforcement by state officials, a novel maneuver aimed at circumventing judicial review, and instead relies on citizens to file legal claims against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers or anyone suspected of ''aiding or abetting'' an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Successful suits can bring the plaintiffs awards of at least $10,000.\par \par \uc2 Proponents of the law and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists had been satisfied that the threat of legal action appeared to stop most abortions in Texas. Some feared that the openness of the law -- allowing anyone to file suit -- could result in a first test case that was unfavorable to their cause.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Stilley said he was not trying to halt abortions by Dr. Alan Braid, a San Antonio physician who wrote in The Washington Post on Saturday that he had violated the Texas law -- which prohibits abortions after cardiac activity is detected, or roughly six weeks into pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not pro-life,'' Mr. Stilley, 58, said in an interview. ''The thing that I'm trying to vindicate here is the law. We pride ourselves on being a nation of laws. What's the law?''\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department has sued Texas over the law, known as Senate Bill 8, and argued in an emergency motion last week that the state adopted the measure ''to prevent women from exercising their constitutional rights.''\par \par \uc2 ''It is settled constitutional law that 'a state may not prohibit any woman from making the ultimate decision to terminate her pregnancy before viability,''' the department said in the lawsuit, referring to the standard set in the 1973 landmark case Roe v. Wade. ''But Texas has done just that.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Braid was also sued on Monday by an Illinois man, Felipe N. Gomez, who described himself in his complaint as a ''pro-choice plaintiff.'' Mr. Gomez could not be immediately reached for comment about his lawsuit, which was earlier reported by KSAT news in San Antonio.\par \par \uc2 Both suits were filed in state court in San Antonio and both men are representing themselves.\par \par \uc2 ''Neither of these lawsuits are valid attempts to save innocent human lives,'' said John Seago, legislative director for Texas Right to Life, the state's largest anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, which lobbied for the new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law. ''Both cases are self-serving legal stunts, abusing the cause of action created in the Texas Heartbeat Act for their own purposes.''\par \par \uc2 He added that he and others at Texas Right to Life ''believe Braid published his Op-Ed intending to attract imprudent lawsuits.''\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group that represents Dr. Braid, said he had not been formally served and declined to make him available for an interview. In a statement, the group's senior counsel, Marc Hearron, said the Texas law ''says that 'any person' can sue over a violation, and we are starting to see that happen, including by out-of-state claimants.''\par \par \uc2 In his opinion essay for The Post, Dr. Braid said he had decided to violate the Texas law, which makes no exceptions for rape or incest, out of a firm belief in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. ''I have daughters, granddaughters and nieces. I believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an essential part of health care,'' he wrote. ''I have spent the past 50 years treating and helping patients. I can't just sit back and watch us return to 1972.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Braid wrote that on the morning of Sept. 6, he had ''provided an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a woman who, though still in her first trimester, was beyond the state's new limit.''\par \par \uc2 After reading that, Mr. Stilley said he decided to file suit. His complaint includes a description of his own legal troubles, which he said included a federal conviction for tax evasion and conspiracy; he was released to home confinement after a decade in prison.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Stilley said in the interview that he believed in a woman's right not to have an unwanted child, and that because his lawsuit was a win-win for him, he rushed to file it.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm going to get an answer either way,'' he said. ''If this is a free-for-all, and it's $10,000, I want my $10,000. And yes, I do aim to collect.''\par \par \uc2 Ruth Graham contributed reporting from Dallas.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Graham contributed reporting from Dallas.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2  rights group Reproaction demonstrating outside the U.S. Supreme Court this month. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210921eh9l0004r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
