\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats Look to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Fight to Motivate Suburban Women\b0\par\par\uc2 By Trip Gabriel \par \uc2 1,804 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Warning of Texas-style laws nationwide, the party believes it can use the issue to turn out suburban women in the Virginia governor's race this fall and the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 VIRGINIA BEACH -- Kenzie Smith is ''not big into politics,'' she said, and while she votes faithfully in presidential elections, for Democrats, she is less interested in off-year races, such as those seven weeks away in Virginia for governor and the legislature.\par \par \uc2 But the recent news that the Supreme Court had allowed Texas to ban most abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy, with no exceptions for rape or incest, grabbed her attention.\par \par \uc2 The fear that such a restrictive law, which she called ''insane,'' could come to Virginia if Republicans take power has sharpened her desire to turn out on Election Day. ''If there are laws like what's going on in Texas coming here, I'd absolutely be motivated to go to the polls over that,'' said Ms. Smith, 33, a marketing consultant.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision on Sept. 1 to let Texas enact the country's most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law came as a grievous blow to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates, a long-sought victory for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents and, for Democrats, a potential political opportunity.\par \par \uc2 As the party mobilizes for next year's midterms, its first big test on the issue will come in the Virginia elections this fall. Democrats are hoping to win a tight governor's race and keep control of the legislature in a state that has moved rapidly to the left. Former Gov. Terry McAuliffe, a Democrat who is running for his old office, has repeatedly promised to be a ''brick wall'' against anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, and has played up his defense of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at a debate last week, on the campaign trail and in fund-raising appeals.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in Virginia and beyond are focusing in particular on suburban women, who played a large role in electing President Biden, but whose broader loyalty to his party is not assured. With Republicans smelling blood in next year's midterm elections as Mr. Biden's approval ratings slip and the economy faces a potential stall over the lingering pandemic, Democrats are looking for issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to overcome their voters' complacency now that Donald J. Trump is gone from office.\par \par \uc2 In more than two dozen interviews in the politically divided city of Virginia Beach, the largest in the state but essentially a patchwork of suburban neighborhoods, Democratic-leaning and independent female voters expressed fear and outrage over the Supreme Court's green light for the Texas law. Many said it intensified their desire to elect Democrats, although historically, single issues have not driven turnout waves; candidate personalities and the overall economy have.\par \par \uc2 Even a number of women who said they favored Republicans noted that they also supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- which may explain why G.O.P. candidates in Virginia have played down the issue, scrubbing anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments from campaign websites and walking back some remarks.\par \par \uc2 In a debate on Thursday between candidates for governor, Glenn Youngkin, the Republican, said, ''I would not sign the Texas bill today.'' But he dodged when asked if he would sign a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban with exceptions for rape and incest. He affirmed that he supported a ''pain-threshold bill,'' which generally outlaws \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Mr. McAuliffe said he was ''terrified'' that ''the Trump Supreme Court'' could overturn Roe v. Wade, the 1973 landmark decision granting a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said he supported ''a woman's right to make her own decision to a second trimester.'' He misleadingly said that Mr. Youngkin ''wants to ban abortions.''\par \par \uc2 Early in the campaign, a liberal activist recorded Mr. Youngkin saying that he had to play down his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views to win over independents, but that if he were elected and Republicans took the House of Delegates, he would start ''going on offense.'' The McAuliffe campaign turned the recording into an attack ad.\par \par \uc2 Republicans portray Mr. McAuliffe as favoring abortions up to the moment of birth, trying to tie him to a failed 2019 bill in the legislature that would have loosened some restrictions on late-term abortions. Virginia law permits abortions in the third trimester if a woman's life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 Polling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shows that Americans' attitudes have remained stable for decades, with a majority of around 60 percent saying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases. In Virginia, slightly fewer people, 55 percent, agree, according to the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, in a contradiction that illustrates the moral complexities of the issue, national polls also show that majorities favor \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that are impermissible under Roe, such as outlawing second-trimester abortions in most cases.\par \par \uc2 A Washington Post-Schar School poll of Virginia conducted this month, after the Supreme Court cleared the way for the Texas law, found that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ranked low among voters' concerns, with only 9 percent saying that it was their most important issue in the governor's race.\par \par \uc2 The starkness of the Texas decision -- and the prospect that the Supreme Court could overturn Roe next year in a case involving a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Mississippi -- has sharpened the issue.\par \par \uc2 Virginia Beach presents a test case of the fraught \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue on the front lines of America's shifting electoral landscape. The large population of military families has long lent a conservative cast to local politics, but last year the city voted for a Democratic presidential candidate, Mr. Biden, for the first time since Lyndon B. Johnson. Representative \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2 , a Democrat and former Navy commander whose congressional district includes Virginia Beach, is among Republicans' top targets for 2022.\par \par \uc2 The city stretches from saltwater taffy shops on the touristy Atlantic beaches to quiet streets of brick homes that lace around the area's many bays. Outdoor conversations are interrupted by earsplitting military jets, which rarely draw a glance skyward.\par \par \uc2 Ellen Robinson, a retired nurse, who identifies as a political independent, was ''horrified'' by the Texas law and said that if the court overturned Roe, ''I think it would be the beginning of fascism in this country.''\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Moran, a technical editor in the engineering field, who favors Democrats, said the Supreme Court's decision on the Texas law ''scared'' her.\par \par \uc2 ''I have boys who will be dating women,'' she said. ''I have nieces. This goes back to the whole 'white men get to make all the decisions about everything.'''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Moran said she was more intent on voting after the court declined to halt the Texas law, which the Biden administration is trying to block.\par \par \uc2 ''We are in a really dangerous situation,'' she said. ''Obviously for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we don't want to become Texas, but on a lot of issues we could lose what is now a blue state.''\par \par \uc2 While many Republican women across Virginia would most likely support stricter \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, few conservative-leaning women in suburban Virginia Beach expressed support for a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law or a reversal of Roe v. Wade. Overall, while these women didn't always embrace the ''pro-choice'' label, they agreed that women should be able to make their own reproductive decisions.\par \par \uc2 ''I know Republicans have been against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forever, but as a woman, I think I ought to be able to choose myself,'' said Janis Cohen, 73, a retired government employee. Her lawn featured a parade of signs for G.O.P. candidates. When it was pointed out that one of them, Winsome Sears, who is running for lieutenant governor, has said she would support a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Ms. Cohen fired back that the current governor, the Democrat Ralph Northam, was what she considered an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremist.\par \par \uc2 In 2019 the governor, a pediatric neurologist, seemed to suggest that a delivered baby could be left to die if the mother requested an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while in labor with a deformed fetus unlikely to survive. Republicans across the country seized on the comments as sanctioning ''infanticide.'' Mr. Northam's office called the accusations a bad-faith distortion of his views.\par \par \uc2 Polls of the Virginia governor's race have generally forecast a close race, including one by Emerson College last week with the candidates within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Guy, a Democratic state delegate who flipped a Republican-held seat in Virginia Beach by just 27 votes in 2019, said that before \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose as an issue in recent weeks, ''most people were complacent and not paying attention.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Guy's opponent has pledged that if elected, he will donate his salary to a so-called crisis pregnancy center that steers pregnant women away from abortions. The contrast could not be more clear to voters who follow the issues. Still, Ms. Guy said, with the news constantly churning, it is difficult to know what will drive voters nearly two months from now to cast ballots.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in Virginia made huge strides during Mr. Trump's divisive leadership, culminating in 2019, when the party took control of both the State Senate and House of Delegates. But Democrats' majorities are slim, and Republicans believe they have an anti-incumbent wind at their backs this year. Three statewide positions are on the ballot on Nov. 2 -- governor, lieutenant governor and attorney general -- along with all 100 seats in the House.\par \par \uc2 The field director for Planned Parenthood Advocates of Virginia said that on average, 10 to 15 volunteers were on door-knocking shifts, compared with 25 to 40 two years ago, a worrying sign for supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Han Jones, Planned Parenthood's political director in Virginia, added: ''People are exhausted with elections and exhausted with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's rhetoric and feel like they can take a break. We could easily go red in this election alone if Democratic voters who are not feeling as passionate or leaned in don't turn out to vote.''\par \par \uc2 A team of Planned Parenthood canvassers who visited a neighborhood of attached town homes recently encountered general support for Democrats, but not much awareness of the election or enthusiasm for it.\par \par \uc2 One voter, Carly White, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a touchy subject in her household. ''I'm for Planned Parenthood but my husband is not,'' she said, stepping outside a home with a small, precisely trimmed lawn. ''I think the issue is, he's a man. He's never grown a baby. I just can't -- I don't like somebody telling me what I can do with my own body.''\par \par \uc2 ormer Gov. Terry McAuliffe, a Virginia Democrat running for his old office, said he'll be a ''brick wall'' on anti-\uc2  measures. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CARLOS BERNATE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A14) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210920eh9k0003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
