\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Clinic Urges Court to Reaffirm Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,004 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a new brief in a major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, a clinic and a doctor asked the court to strike down a state law largely banning the procedure after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Mississippi urged the Supreme Court on Monday to reaffirm Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The filing came in the most important \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case in decades, in which officials in Mississippi have asked the court's newly expanded conservative majority to overrule Roe and to sustain a state law that largely bans abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi's arguments, the brief said, amounted to ''a request that the court scuttle a half-century of precedent and invite states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  entirely.''\par \par \uc2 The providers' brief came less than two weeks after a closely divided Supreme Court refused to block a Texas law that banned most abortions after six weeks, one that had been drafted to evade challenges to it in federal court. But the ruling in the Texas case was procedural and provisional, while the Mississippi filing came in a case presenting a frontal challenge to Roe and the decisions that followed it.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , the president of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which represents the providers, said the Mississippi case had the potential to do away with the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in much of the nation.\par \par \uc2 ''Right now,'' she said, ''Texas has managed to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even as Roe stands, and other states have said they will follow in its footsteps. While Texas is circumventing Roe and the Constitution, Mississippi is openly asking the court to overturn Roe. If the court grants Mississippi's request to overturn Roe, large swaths of the South and Midwest -- where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already hard to access -- will eliminate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  completely.''\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Lynn Fitch of Mississippi, a Republican, said that the brief offered ''no solid arguments in defense of Roe, and the court should overturn this flawed and hopelessly unworkable precedent.''\par \par \uc2 The court has not yet scheduled the argument in the case, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, No. 19-1392, though sometime in December would seem to be a good bet.\par \par \uc2 The new brief, filed on behalf of the state's only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic and its medical director, said the Supreme Court had already considered and rejected Mississippi's arguments against Roe in other cases. The most notable is Planned Parenthood v. Casey, the 1992 decision that reaffirmed Roe's core holding and said states may not impose an ''undue burden'' on the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability, the point at which fetuses can sustain life outside the womb, or about 23 or 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The law at issue in the new case was enacted in 2018 by the Republican-dominated Mississippi Legislature. It banned abortions if ''the probable gestational age of the unborn human'' was determined to be more than 15 weeks. The statute included narrow exceptions for medical emergencies or ''a severe fetal abnormality.''\par \par \uc2 Lower courts blocked the Mississippi statute, calling it a cynical and calculated assault on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights directly at odds with Supreme Court precedents. The justices agreed to hear the case in May, just months after Justice Amy Coney Barrett, who has said she personally opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , joined the court. She replaced Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , a proponent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, who died last year.\par \par \uc2 In their petition seeking Supreme Court review, state officials told the justices that ''the questions presented in this petition do not require the court to overturn Roe or Casey,'' though they did raise the possibility in a footnote. Once the court agreed to hear the case, the officials shifted their emphasis and began a sustained assault on those precedents.\par \par \uc2 In a brief filed in July, Ms. Fitch wrote that Roe was ''egregiously wrong.''\par \par \uc2 ''The Constitution does not protect a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' she wrote. ''The Constitution's text says nothing about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nothing in the Constitution's structure implies a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or prohibits states from restricting it.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fitch told the justices that the scope of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights should be determined through the political process. ''The national fever on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can break,'' she wrote, ''only when this court returns \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to the states -- where agreement is more common, compromise is often possible, and disagreement can be resolved at the ballot box.''\par \par \uc2 In the brief filed on Monday, the Mississippi \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers suggested that the change in the state's approach represented a bait-and-switch tactic that could warrant dismissal of the case.\par \par \uc2 The precise question the justices agreed to decide was ''whether all previability prohibitions on elective abortions are unconstitutional.'' Depending on how the court answers that question, it could reaffirm, revise or do away with the longstanding constitutional framework for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The providers devoted much of their brief to a defense of viability as a principled and workable dividing line between when states can ban abortions and when they cannot.\par \par \uc2 The line has worked in practice, the brief said. ''Federal courts have applied the viability rule with remarkable uniformity and predictability for five decades,'' it said, ''finding previability bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  invalid regardless of whether those bans operated at six, 12 or 20 weeks and regardless of the reasons states alleged to justify them.''\par \par \uc2 Mississippi has not come forward with a good alternative to viability, the providers wrote. ''A party asking this court to take the grave step of overruling a rule of law -- one that has been repeatedly reaffirmed -- should at least propose and seriously develop an alternative legal framework,'' the brief said.\par \par \uc2 The text mentioned the Texas law only in passing, as a foreshadowing of the chaos and upheaval that it said would follow a decision overruling Roe.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \uc2  rights this month at the Supreme Court. Officials in Mississippi have asked for Roe to be overturned. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210914eh9e0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
