\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Ordinary Citizens Can Act as Bounty Hunters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan Feuer \par \uc2 1,123 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 There is little precedent for the provision that deputizes ordinary citizens to enforce an effective ban on abortions -- and offers them a financial incentive to do so.\par \par \uc2 The new law in Texas effectively banning most abortions has ignited widespread controversy and debate, in part because of the mechanism it uses to enforce the restrictions: deputizing ordinary people to sue those involved in performing abortions and giving them a financial incentive to do so.\par \par \uc2 The law establishes a kind of bounty system. If these vigilante plaintiffs are successful, the law allows them to collect cash judgments of $10,000 -- and their legal fees -- from those they sue. If they lose, they do not have to pay the defendants' legal costs.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court declined to stop the legislation from taking effect, and so far, no one has brought a suit against an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider because clinics in the state have chosen to abide by the law, which effectively bars abortions starting around the sixth week of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The enforcement provision has generated backing from those seeking to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights but confusion and criticism among supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''When the law first came out and I was reading it, I thought I was missing something,'' said Mary Ziegler, a professor at the Florida State University College of Law who specializes in the history of reproductive law. ''It almost seemed like anyone could sue anyone -- and that didn't seem right. But it was. It really is that extraordinary.''\par \par \uc2 Here are some questions and answers about the enforcement provision.\par \par \uc2 Are there other laws that use the same mechanism?\par \par \uc2 Not really -- or at least not with the same scope as the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 While giving private citizens the right to file suit in lieu of -- or in tandem with -- criminal enforcement by state officials is a staple of medical malpractice cases and other forms of tort law, the Texas law is different.\par \par \uc2 It removes enforcement entirely from state jurisdiction, and vastly expands who can sue, and who can be sued, over abortions. The statute, for example, permits anyone -- even people who live outside Texas -- to file a complaint in any court in the state if they believe an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been performed. It also makes nearly everyone involved in the procedure -- except for the woman who receives the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- liable to suits, meaning that doctors, nurses, insurance companies, even Uber drivers who help take women to clinics, could be vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 According to Ms. Ziegler, the notion of using civil lawsuits to curb or stop abortions first emerged in the early 1990s, when a Texas pastor named Mark Crutcher created a program called Spies for Life that published manuals showing people how to use the legal system to go after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and providers. In 1999, Louisiana passed its own law giving women who had abortions the right to sue their providers.\par \par \uc2 State Senator \uc2 Bryan Hughes\uc2 , the primary author of the Texas law, has said his model for the law, known as S. B. 8, was a local ordinance passed in Waskom, Texas, in 2019 that empowered residents to sue anyone who performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the city or helped someone attain one. Unlike S.B. 8, however, the Waskom law was largely symbolic, given that the city had no clinics that actually performed abortions.\par \par \uc2 What legal issues does private enforcement raise?\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department sued Texas on Thursday, arguing that S. B. 8 was passed ''in open defiance of the Constitution'' and Supreme Court cases like Planned Parenthood v. Casey and Roe v. Wade. But the department's 27-page complaint took particular issue with the law's reliance on what it called ''bounty hunters,'' saying that empowering them to enforce the law was an ''unprecedented scheme to insulate the state from responsibility.''\par \par \uc2 Moreover, officials claimed, S. B. 8 had essentially frozen the practice of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas and achieved its goal of stopping the procedures without a single private lawsuit having been filed. After all, the complaint pointed out, the mere threat of litigation was enough ''to make it too risky for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic to operate'' in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The department's legal case relies on the argument that ordinary people, if and when they do file suit against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, will in effect be acting as agents of the state of Texas. What the government is asking for in its complaint amounts to a federal injunction barring everyone in the entire state from filing suits against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, which some lawyers say could be a bit far-fetched. Then again, it may not be any more far-fetched than S. B. 8 itself, which empowered everyone in the entire state to file suit.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, legal scholars said, S. B. 8 is also likely to be challenged in another way. At some point, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider or someone else involved in the process -- say, a group that funds abortions -- could step forward and willingly violate the law as a calculated test case. But that could take time and have uncertain results.\par \par \uc2 ''Whatever happens, it's going to take a while,'' Ms. Ziegler said. ''And in the meantime, this law will be the status quo.''\par \par \uc2 Are other states adopting similar laws?\par \par \uc2 In recent days, lawmakers and executives in at least seven states have said they are considering similar statutes. Last week, \uc2 Gov. Kristi L. Noem\uc2  of South Dakota, a Republican, said she had directed lawyers in her office to review S. B. 8 ''to make sure we have the strongest pro-life laws on the books.'' Around the same time, \uc2 Wilton Simpson\uc2 , the Republican leader of the Florida State Senate, said that members of his chamber were already working on a statute similar to the one in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The private enforcement mechanism was also inserted into a new gun law in Missouri that grants residents the power to sue local law departments for giving ''material aid and support'' to federal agents for perceived violations of the Second Amendment. Under the law, which the Justice Department has challenged, Missourians could file suit to stop local police officers from sharing data or conducting joint operations with federal agents working on gun cases.\par \par \uc2 While some on the left have talked about the notion of drafting laws empowering people to sue over issues they hold dear -- like stricter gun regulations, for example -- it has not risen much so far beyond social media chatter. For now at least, Democrats have not seized on private civil enforcement as a legal strategy.\par \par \uc2 Demonstrators rallying for \uc2  rights outside the Supreme Court in Washington on Thursday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Kenny Holston for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210912eh9c0004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
