\par\par \uc2 THE INTERPRETER\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Expand, U.S. Joins the Telling Exceptions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Max Fisher \par \uc2 1,442 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Recent shifts on access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggest democracy and women's rights go hand in hand -- and that the inverse might be true as well.\par \par \uc2 The story of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the 21st century can be seen in two world-shaking developments this past week.\par \par \uc2 In the first, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  effectively upheld drastic new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in Texas. A few days later, Mexico's high court paved the way for nationwide legalization.\par \par \uc2 It may be tempting to see Mexico's ruling as the more surprising, catapulting the world's second most populous Catholic country on a deeply contentious social matter.\par \par \uc2 But experts say it is the United States that stands out. Since 2000, 31 countries, many just as pious as Mexico, have expanded access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Only three have rolled it back: Nicaragua, Poland and the United States.\par \par \uc2 Parallels between the United States and Mexico run deep. Polarized public opinion. Fiercely committed women's rights groups on one side and religious groups on the other. Federal systems enabling a patchwork of state-level laws. High courts with histories of intervening.\par \par \uc2 If anything, the United States would seem the likelier to widen access. Its public opinion is significantly more supportive. It has precedent in Roe v. Wade and, as a result of that 1973 decision, a 48-year-old cultural norm around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The two countries' divergence illustrates the advancement and backlash that now drive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics worldwide.\par \par \uc2 It is a story defined by the collision of larger forces, often linked to a defining issue of our time: democracy's rise and its retrenchment.\par \par \uc2 A rough but reliable rule has emerged, said Sonia Corr\u234\'00\'EAa, a prominent women's rights researcher. Where democracy expands, women's rights follow, of which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is often one. But the inverse may be true, too.\par \par \uc2 That trend has accelerated, she said, but so has a backlash, often tied to rising nationalism and right-wing populism, that has intensified in the last 20 years.\par \par \uc2 A Global Struggle\par \par \uc2 The liberalizing trend, from Britain's \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act, passed in 1967, through Mexico's ruling this week, has usually followed a pattern.\par \par \uc2 A women's rights movement will arise somewhere, often as part of democratization, in which such groups may play a prominent role. Medical groups and United Nations agencies might voice support. Public opinion on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soften.\par \par \uc2 A partial or local-level legalization will prove popular, as happened in Mexico, paving the way to more. The legislature or high court, perhaps bowing to public pressure, will step in.\par \par \uc2 And each breakthrough will inspire others. Mexico's campaigners wore green handkerchiefs, a nod to Argentine activists who pushed successfully for legalization last year.\par \par \uc2 ''Seeing what they've done in Latin America, 10 years ago we'd have thought it was impossible,'' said Serra Sippel, the president of the Center for Health and Gender Equity.\par \par \uc2 But traditional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents like the Vatican and evangelicals, after years of losing ground, have found new allies.\par \par \uc2 Nationalist leaders have stirred up social resentments and won over religious groups by targeting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights campaigners -- often as part of a broader crackdown on civil society.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. reversal, in a wealthy democracy with long-held \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, is an even greater outlier, said Elizabeth Heger Boyle, a gender rights scholar at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Though most Americans support legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an entrenched minority remains.\par \par \uc2 Partisanship is one factor, locking in opposition among demographics that, in other countries, have softened their views.\par \par \uc2 Still, in most countries, forces like partisanship or nationalism only slow the expansion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. It takes something more drastic to roll it back.\par \par \uc2 Minority Rule\par \par \uc2 High courts are generally thought to incorporate public opinion on contentious social matters. Mexico's is an example: It jumped ahead of public opinion on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but in a direction that Mexicans were slowly trending.\par \par \uc2 But last week's United States ruling may be symptomatic, some political scientists argue, of a significant change in democracy there and elsewhere. Its major institutions increasingly empower minority rule.\par \par \uc2 ''Thirty-five, 40 percent of the electorate,'' said Steven Levitsky, a \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  scholar on democracy, ''now can be enough, given the electoral system,'' to win power.\par \par \uc2 Electoral College and Senate maps have always tilted American elections to favor certain voters over others, for instance by granting rural states outsized representation. For the first time in American history, demographic groups that tend to support one party, the G.O.P., overwhelmingly cluster in the areas that receive disproportionate voice.\par \par \uc2 As a result, Supreme Court justices are increasingly likely to be appointed by a president who lost the popular vote and confirmed by a Senate elected by a minority. Republicans won the national popular vote in only one out of the last eight presidential elections, but have appointed six of the nine current Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 In democracies, a drift toward minority rule can feed a sense that power does not flow from the will of the people as a whole. Such leaders and institutions often become likelier to overrule the majority on issues important to the minority that put them in power.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, partisan combat has grown more intense, with studies finding that Republicans are likelier to breach democratic norms, including in blocking then-President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  from filling a Supreme Court vacancy in 2016.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a lot of hardball involved in creating this six out of nine conservative majority,'' Dr. Levitsky said.\par \par \uc2 In societies with high polarization, he has found, parties often fight bitterly for control of the courts. These contests tend to send a message, intended or not, that courts exist to serve partisan interests, rather than guard against them.\par \par \uc2 Rulings at odds with public opinion, Dr. Levitsky said, can become ''very likely in a period of polarization and hardball politics.''\par \par \uc2 This may help explain why all three countries to roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights this century -- Nicaragua, Poland and the United States -- did so amid bare-knuckle fights for control of the high court.\par \par \uc2 Outliers\par \par \uc2 The only two developed countries to roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the United States and Poland, share a revealingly similar trajectory.\par \par \uc2 In both, high courts rolled back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that were favored by national majorities.\par \par \uc2 And both rulings were preceded by the rise of populist leaders who widened social divisions and promised to smash or co-opt independent institutions.\par \par \uc2 Conservative groups have long sought to overturn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. But they have been ''radicalized'' by the populist surge, Dr. Levitsky said, of voters who see themselves as besieged minorities fighting for the survival of their way of life.\par \par \uc2 Though Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restriction came through normal procedure -- albeit one that some critics consider legally dubious for its open effort to sidestep judicial oversight -- it hints at a broader phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 Curbs on women's rights tend to accelerate in backsliding democracies, a category that includes the United States, according to virtually every independent metric and watchdog.\par \par \uc2 In more degraded democracies, the effect is more extreme. Around the globe, the rise of right-wing populism has been followed by extraordinary reductions in women's rights, according to a 2019 report by Freedom House.\par \par \uc2 Strongmen often curb civil society as a whole, of which women's groups tend to be leading members. And they rise on appeals to nationalism, with its calls for rigid social hierarchies and mores.\par \par \uc2 ''There is a trend to watch for in countries that have not necessarily successfully rolled it back, but are introducing legislation to roll it back,'' Rebecca Turkington, a \uc2 University of Cambridge\uc2  scholar, said of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, ''in that this is part of a broader crackdown on women's rights. And that goes hand in hand with creeping authoritarianism.''\par \par \uc2 For all the complexities around the ebb and flow of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, a simple formula holds surprisingly widely. Majoritarianism and the rights of women, the only universal majority, are inextricably linked. Where one rises or falls, so does the other.\par \par \uc2 Women celebrating in Saltillo, Mexico, on Tuesday after the Supreme Court there decriminalized \uc2  in the world's second most populous Catholic country. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DANIEL BECERRIL/REUTERS); Protesting near the U.S. Supreme Court last week. Since 2000, 31 countries have expanded access to \uc2 . Only Nicaragua, Poland and the U.S. have cut back. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); A protest in January against Poland's new anti-\uc2  law after its high court restricted rights favored by the majority. The U.S. has acted in a similar fashion. (PHOTOGRAPH BY LESZEK SZYMANSKI/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210910eh9a0004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
