\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Protecting A Child Isn't 'Extreme'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Swallow Prior \par \uc2 961 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a failure for every woman and her unborn child -- a failure of love, justice and mercy. Texas' new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law is far from perfect, but I hope it can move us closer to these ideals.\par \par \uc2 The highest purpose of human law is the protection of human life, from its beginning to its natural end. As a pro-life Christian, I believe that each of our individual origins are in the moment of conception. That's when my life and your life began. Not in some abstract, ethereal way, but for real -- all the very particular DNA, chromosomes, eye color, hair texture and toes of you.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law doesn't ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from this earliest beginning. Rather it bans almost all abortions after cardiac activity in the unborn child is detected, usually around six weeks into pregnancy, when cheeks, chin and jaws are also starting to form.\par \par \uc2 Because most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures in Texas take place after six weeks, the new law, as one of the nation's most restrictive, will certainly reduce abortions in Texas drastically. It will also continue to be challenged in the courts. But if we start from the biological and ontological reality that each human life begins at conception, the law is hardly ''extreme,'' as President Biden has called it. A law preserving the life of a human being at any stage can be considered ''extreme'' only within a distorted social context.\par \par \uc2 Because of the challenges Roe v. Wade has presented to attempts by states over the years to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in any meaningful way (such as after the first trimester, a restriction most Americans favor), the Texas law doesn't criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright. Rather, it allows private citizens to sue providers and others through civil litigation. Successful suits may result in fines hefty enough to put many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices out of business, an innovative workaround.\par \par \uc2 Deputizing private citizens to enforce the ban is certainly fraught with risk. Some fear the law will turn otherwise uninterested citizens into bounty hunters who chase \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, although one judge's temporary restraining order that blocks one group from suing Planned Parenthood shows that checks and balances are in place.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the most pivotal part of this law is that it creates a cause of action against anyone who ''aids or abets'' the performance or inducement of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it doesn't fully define ''aids or abets.''\par \par \uc2 Thus, the law demands us to ask: What does it really mean to offer aid to someone to whom \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems the best option?\par \par \uc2 In America, of all the pregnancies that don't end in miscarriage, nearly one in five is aborted; this is a society in which things are wildly off track. A world like this, spun by forces that lead to that many lives being undone, doesn't happen by chance. It takes all of us.\par \par \uc2 It takes a village to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seem like the best choice.\par \par \uc2 We can change our ways, though. The Texas law ought to compel us to help women with unwanted pregnancies in meaningful ways. The millions of dollars that Texas lawmakers have allocated to the Alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, which offers support to those who ''choose life in difficult circumstances,'' is a start. But Texas' infamously high maternal mortality rate must also be addressed. It is not pro-life to save the child, only to lose her mother in the process. Unborn children don't exist in a vacuum. Their lives are conceived, birthed (or not) and lived in community. To serve them, we must serve their communities.\par \par \uc2 A few years ago, following another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  controversy, a pastor argued in a since-viral \uc2 Facebook\uc2  post that '' 'the unborn' are a convenient group of people to advocate for,'' because they don't make demands on us. Unlike others who need help, they don't disagree or argue or require that we change our lifestyles or societal structures. They are silent. As long as they live in their mother's womb, defending their lives requires no sacrifice from us.\par \par \uc2 This idea challenges me. It should challenge us all, including those who think other things outweigh letting little embryonic girls and boys to continue to live. A world that pits a mother's well-being against her child's life is world that needs extensive repair.\par \par \uc2 And the fact is that in our consumerism, individualism and pragmatism, we live in a transactional society that gives rise to bounty hunters on the left and on the right. Outside the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, and inside them, too.\par \par \uc2 We need to be people who act (not transact) for mercy, justice, and love.\par \par \uc2 And love isn't love that doesn't act.\par \par \uc2 As history has shown again and again, we sometimes need the law to teach us to love. Sometimes it takes a law to remind us that fellow human beings are not ours to own, harm, or kill.\par \par \uc2 Love is a higher law. But it is still a law. And this is where we must begin.\par \par \uc2 Karen Swallow Prior is a research professor at Southeastern Baptist Theological Seminary and a columnist at the Religion News Service. She is the author of ''On Reading Well: Finding the Good Life in Great Books.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Claire Merchlinsky for The New York Times; Photographs by Garen Meguerian\\Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210910eh9a0003a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
