\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Little Access to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  in Mexico Despite Court Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalie Kitroeff and Oscar Lopez \par \uc2 1,468 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision set a legal precedent for the nation. But applying it to all of Mexico's states will be a long path, and women are still facing prosecution.\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY -- When the Supreme Court in Mexico issued a historic decision on Tuesday declaring that having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not a crime, activists across the country celebrated. On Wednesday, they got back to work, taking on the long and arduous process of ensuring that the legal shift applies across Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Among their top priorities are helping the women who need it most: those facing criminal penalties, often after having been reported to the authorities for trying to induce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  themselves under dangerous conditions.\par \par \uc2 ''A woman who decides to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already vulnerable, and then we also have to confront the dire situation of believing we will face punishment,'' said Yetlanezi Pech, who was rushed to a hospital, bleeding, after an attempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , only to have an emergency room doctor deny her help and turn her in to authorities.\par \par \uc2 ''I felt so much fear, I felt so unsafe, I felt really, really bad,'' she said. ''And I also felt alone.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Pech is among the thousands of women who have been investigated for illegally obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent years. In the first seven months of this year alone, 432 investigations were opened across Mexico into cases of illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the Mexican government.\par \par \uc2 The ruling on Tuesday set a legal precedent for the nation -- and stands in stark contrast to the trend in the United States, where Texas and other states have recently moved to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The court's decision also raised the prospect of Mexico eventually becoming a destination for American women seeking to end their pregnancies, advocates said, though that would require removing the many obstacles that make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  difficult to obtain in much of the country.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's decision applies only to the border state of Coahuila, and putting it into practice nationwide requires either legal challenges in each of the 28 states in Mexico that still criminalize the procedure, or a change in law by state legislatures. The justices did not specify how far into a pregnancy a woman can legally obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meaning those terms will likely be determined at the state level.\par \par \uc2 A leading \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group in Mexico, GIRE, said that it would push for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal in Coahuila for 12 weeks after conception at a minimum -- a time limit established in the law that made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal in Mexico City and which was previously validated by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 If that were the case, Coahuila would have more permissive abortions rules than neighboring Texas, where the state legislature recently implemented a law that prohibits most abortions after about six weeks. In time, women from Texas could potentially cross the border to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- but for now, there wouldn't be enough infrastructure in place to meet the need, activists said.\par \par \uc2 ''There is more to be done before women and people who can get pregnant in Texas are able to get the service in Coahuila,'' said Melissa Ayala Garc\u237\'00\'EDa, coordinator of litigation for GIRE. ''We still have a ways to go to ensure the service is provided.''\par \par \uc2 The Texas law bars abortions once cardiac activity can be detected in the embryo. There is no heart at this stage of development, only electrical activity in developing cells that starts at around six weeks, before many women are even aware they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Activists in Mexico have already begun working on a strategy to force states to fall in line with the court's ruling, though their fight to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal and safe across the country could be a long one. Only Mexico City, and three other states, allowed abortions on request before Tuesday's decision.\par \par \uc2 ''We are already organized and ready to take advantage of the opportunity that the court's new decision offers,'' said Arely Torres Miranda, a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocate in the state of San Luis Potos\u237\'00\'ED. ''What we need to do is make them change the law.''\par \par \uc2 That plan is likely to meet resistance. Mexico's conservative PAN party, one of the main opposition parties, has fought efforts to legalize the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''The party clearly has both in its platform and in its principles the right to life from conception,'' said Dami\u225\'00\'E1n Zepeda Vidales, a PAN senator and former party leader.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zepeda, who said he would personally oppose legalization efforts in the Senate, added that the party was waiting until the Supreme Court published the decision behind Tuesday's ruling to determine its national strategy.\par \par \uc2 Since Mexico City legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2007, a network of activists based there has worked to offer women seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a safe path, either by transporting them to the capital or providing them misoprostol, a drug commonly used to induce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But many women are too scared to reach out to those groups and opt to obtain clandestine abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''We have seen terrible cases where they do it with coat hangers, where they hit their bellies,'' Ms. Torres Miranda said. ''They put their lives at risk.''\par \par \uc2 When those methods go wrong or lead to excessive bleeding, women often go to hospitals. But federal law requires medical providers to notify authorities when a patient presents signs of having been involved in criminal activity -- such as having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Yesterday's ruling will also allow us, when we modify the law, to remove the obligation for the health sector to report when they discover an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Ms. Torres Miranda said.\par \par \uc2 Generally, the women who are most marginalized -- who are poor and live in rural areas -- are the ones who face criminal penalties for having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ms. Torres Miranda said.\par \par \uc2 It was not clear how many women in total are currently facing prosecution or are in prison for having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , activists said, because comprehensive state-level data has been difficult to gather.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Pech said that as soon as she got to the emergency room after her at-home \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  went wrong two years ago, she was treated as though she did not deserve medical attention. The doctor who admitted Ms. Pech accused her of being a drug addict and persuaded her colleagues not to offer any help.\par \par \uc2 ''No one wanted to come near me because I was in the process of having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Ms. Pech said. When she was finally taken to surgery, she said, her partner at the time was told that both of them would soon be detained for the crime of ending the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''The same doctor who received me called the authorities to come arrest me,'' she said. She was able to quickly hire a lawyer and avoid arrest. But before the lawyer agreed to fight the case in court, he came to her house to deliver an ultimatum.\par \par \uc2 ''He told me I had to ask God for forgiveness for what I had done,'' she said. ''And then he would help me.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Pech, who has two sons, said that with the lawyer's assistance she managed to avoid facing criminal penalties.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's landmark decision is the first of several cases on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to be taken up by the Supreme Court this month. On Thursday, justices will examine whether a provision in a law in Sinaloa state that protects life from conception is similarly unconstitutional. Given the court's recent rulings in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, analysts say it is very likely that the judges will rule to strike the law.\par \par \uc2 ''That would also be historic,'' said Ms. Ayala, the litigation coordinator for GIRE. ''We are in a very, very, very important September.''\par \par \uc2 But among the general population, the issue remains divisive: Mexico is a largely socially conservative country where the Catholic church holds significant sway. A majority of Mexicans are still opposed to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , polling shows.\par \par \uc2 But attitudes have shifted over time. In 2005, just 12 percent of the country was in favor of legalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all cases, according to a poll from the research firm Parametria. A 2019 poll conducted by the El Financiero newspaper found that almost one-third of Mexicans said they were in favor of full legalization.\par \par \uc2 This week's decision signaled, for Ms. Pech at least, that change may finally come, and other women may be spared the suffering she experienced.\par \par \uc2 ''This opens the possibility that these things won't happen anymore,'' she said. ''There's no reason this has to happen.''\par \par \uc2 A rally in Saltillo, Mexico, on Tuesday celebrating a Supreme Court decision declaring that having an \uc2  is not a crime. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DANIEL BECERRIL/REUTERS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210909eh9900044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
