\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justice Dept. Will 'Protect' Right to Seek An  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aishvarya Kavi \par \uc2 871 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The statement from Attorney General \uc2 Merrick B. Garland\uc2  did not directly challenge a new Texas law that banned nearly all abortions in the state.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General \uc2 Merrick B. Garland\uc2  said on Monday that the Justice Department would continue to protect women who seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas, days after a state law enacting a near-complete ban on the procedure went in effect.\par \par \uc2 ''We will not tolerate violence against those seeking to obtain or provide \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  services,'' Mr. Garland said in a statement. ''The department will provide support from federal law enforcement when an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic or \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  center is under attack.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garland said the Justice Department would ''protect the constitutional rights of women and other persons'' under the Freedom of Access to Clinic Entrances Act, or FACE, a 1994 federal law that guarantees access to the entrances of clinics that offer \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, including those that offer abortions. Under the law, it is illegal to threaten, obstruct or injure a person seeking access to such a clinic or to damage the clinic's property, punishable by a fine or imprisonment.\par \par \uc2 In his statement, Mr. Garland did not directly challenge the Texas law, known as Senate Bill 8, which is the most restrictive in the nation and bans all abortions when cardiac activity can be detected. That is usually around six weeks into the pregnancy and before many women know that they are pregnant. The law includes no exceptions for pregnancies caused by rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garland's statement appeared to be an initial step by the Biden administration to find a way to counter a state law that has pushed the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights back into the center of American politics.\par \par \uc2 ''I think he was trying to send a signal to people on the ground that they need to physically leave clinics alone. I think there's a concern that, from reports on the ground, activists are hanging out at the clinics and they're watching who goes in and out,'' said Howard M. Wasserman, a law professor at Florida International University in Miami. ''There may be some intimidation going on, and so FACE would be a way to stop that.''\par \par \uc2 Senate Bill 8 prohibits state officials from enforcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, a feature intended to make it difficult to challenge in the courts. Instead, it effectively deputizes ordinary citizens to sue anyone who performs the procedure or ''aids and abets'' it. Plaintiffs are entitled to at least $10,000 if they are successful.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Greg Abbott signed the measure into law in May. It went into effect on Wednesday after the Supreme Court, in a 5-to-4 vote, decided not to block it, despite the precedent of Roe v. Wade, the 1973 Supreme Court decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Though the majority stressed that it was not ruling on the constitutionality of the Texas law, the decision appeared to confirm the worst fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, who have warned that conservatives were moving aggressively to put in place a court majority that would upend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garland's statement did not go into detail on what support from federal law enforcement for those seeking abortions would look like, though he said that the Justice Department had reached out to the F.B.I. and U.S. attorney's offices in Texas and around the country ''to discuss our enforcement authorities.''\par \par \uc2 President Biden, in a statement last week, slammed the Texas law as ''an unprecedented assault on a woman's constitutional rights.'' He pointed to the law's provision allowing citizens to sue those who violate the statute, saying ''complete strangers will now be empowered to inject themselves in the most private and personal health decisions faced by women.''\par \par \uc2 In response to a question from a reporter, Mr. Biden said on Friday that his administration was exploring federal action to counteract the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 ''I was told that there are possibilities within the existing law to have the Justice Department look and see whether there are things that can be done that can limit the independent action of individuals,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Earlier last week, Mr. Biden directed the Office of the White House Council and federal agencies, including the Justice Department and the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , to look into steps the federal government could take to protect ''access to safe and legal abortions.''\par \par \uc2 While the enforcement of the FACE Act may ''improve the situation on the ground,'' Mr. Wasserman said the Biden administration was ''going to have to find a different way to get'' at the larger problem the Texas bill posed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights: the ability for any citizen to sue an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic or anyone else who assists with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In his statement, Mr. Garland added that the Justice Department was still ''urgently'' exploring ''all options'' to challenge the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 Protesters march outside of the Supreme Court on Saturday, after the court declined to block a new Texas law banning most abortions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Kenny Holston for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210907eh970003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
