\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Behind a Drive In Texas to Halt Most Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ruth Graham \par \uc2 1,740 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Texans are almost evenly divided on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but a combination of Republican control, conservative judicial appointments and cultural shifts helped the state's anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement find success.\par \par \uc2 RICHARDSON, Texas -- A steady stream of women trickled into Prestonwood Pregnancy Center late last week, alone and with partners, with appointments and without. One couple held hands and whispered cheerfully; a young woman scrolled through her phone until her name was called. A wall-mounted screen in a corner cycled through a carousel of inspirational messages. ''You are strong.'' ''Hope is stronger than fear.'' ''There are options.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics emptied out last week after a Texas law enacting a near-complete ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  went into effect. But Prestonwood is not one of those clinics. It is instead among the state's more than 200 ''crisis pregnancy centers,'' facilities aligned with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations that offer free medical tests and counseling in hopes of dissuading women from terminating their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 These centers are sometimes located within eyesight of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, and there are nearly 10 times as many of them, a sign of the extraordinary success of the state's anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement that led to the passage of the country's most restrictive law.\par \par \uc2 How Texas arrived at this moment was the culmination of years of Republican control, conservative judicial appointments and rising passion around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues by many Christians in the state. Polls show Texans almost evenly divided on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and the state's cities have grown more Democratic, but it was the conservative \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents who established a powerful political, cultural and even physical presence across the state's vast terrain.\par \par \uc2 In the race among conservative states to undo the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- as established in 1973 by the landmark case, Roe v. Wade -- Texas ''feels an obligation to lead and be bold,'' said John Seago, legislative director for Texas Right to Life, the state's largest anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, because Roe v. Wade began in Texas, after an unmarried woman challenged the state's criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, the modern anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement in the state has felt compelled to push for its disintegration.\par \par \uc2 ''We started this moral tragedy, it's ours to end,'' said Chelsey Youman, the Texas State Director and National Legislative Advisor for Human Coalition Action, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group based in Frisco, Texas, near Dallas.\par \par \uc2 The modern anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement in Texas, however, did not begin with Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 ''It did not immediately set off a reaction in Texas,'' said Daniel K. Williams, a professor of history at the University of West Georgia. ''For several decades, the issue really didn't have much staying power.''\par \par \uc2 At the time, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause was primarily one pressed by Catholics, and it was strongest in Northern states with large Catholic populations.\par \par \uc2 Evangelicals, dominant in the South, were largely moderate on the issue, generally opposing ''on-demand'' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but open to a variety of exceptions. A 1969 poll by the Baptist Standard found that 90 percent of Texas Baptists thought the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws were too restrictive. W.A. Criswell, a prominent Southern Baptist pastor in Dallas and former president of the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 , remarked in reaction to Roe v. Wade, ''I have always felt that it was only after a child was born and had a life separate from its mother that it became an individual person.'' (By the end of the 1970s, though, Mr. Criswell opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.)\par \par \uc2 Over the decades, Texans continued to elect senators who favored \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the early 21st century. But by then, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement had become a powerful force in evangelical culture.\par \par \uc2 And the state, once solidly in Democratic hands, shifted to Republican control in the mid-1990s. Since 2003, Republicans have held majorities in both chambers of the state legislature and the governor's office. It is also covered by a conservative federal appeals court, the Fifth Circuit in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 Like many other states across the South and Midwest, Texas has steadily chipped away at legal and practical access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for decades, including requiring pregnant women to undergo a sonogram -- by the same doctor who will perform the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- at least 24 hours before the procedure. Other measures, such as the Alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, have helped fund pregnancy centers like Prestonwood, which was founded three decades ago as a ministry of Prestonwood Baptist Church, a nearby evangelical megachurch.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, there are about 24 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Texas, down from 40 less than a decade ago, an imbalance to the scores of pregnancy centers like Prestonwood that speaks to the cultural and political success of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, even as the state's largest and bluest cities get larger and more progressive. The number of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics is sure to drop further, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates said, as many will be forced to close if the new law remains in place.\par \par \uc2 In an emergency application asking the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to intervene, a coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers warned that the law -- which bans abortions at the point when cardiac activity is detected, generally about six weeks, when many women don't yet know they are pregnant -- ''would immediately and catastrophically reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Texas.'' Clinics raced to see clients until the minute the law went into effect last week.\par \par \uc2 In a 5-4 decision, the Supreme Court declined to immediately block the law. The law uses an innovative approach designed to make it more difficult to challenge in court, barring state officials from enforcing it and instead deputizing private individuals to sue anyone who performs the procedure or ''aids and abets'' it. They are entitled to $10,000 and their legal fees covered if they win.\par \par \uc2 Legal parries to blunt its impact continued after its implementation. On Friday evening, a judge issued a temporary restraining order against Texas Right to Life, Mr. Seago's group, blocking it from suing Planned Parenthood until Sept. 17. And the law, which bans abortions after six weeks and makes no exceptions for rape or incest, is still being challenged in the lower federal courts.\par \par \uc2 Texans, like the country at large, remain deeply divided on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In a poll taken in February by the University of Texas and The Texas Tribune, 32 percent of Texans said they wanted to see more restrictive anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in the state, while 37 percent wanted less strict laws.\par \par \uc2 But across the state, in its biggest cities and in rural pockets, it has been the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement that has gained the most momentum over the years. Many churches sponsor related ministries, and a recent calendar of events published by an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  website includes rallies, retreats for men who suffer from the ''repressed grief, anger and guilt'' of terminating a pregnancy, annual walks and runs and hikes. Billboards denouncing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with graphic images, have dotted the highways. (Over the last year, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights billboards have also popped up.)\par \par \uc2 About a decade ago, Texas Right to Life shifted its legal strategy to focus on laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than regulations that chipped away at access. It also decided to pursue legislation founded on the idea that the state had an interest in protecting fetal life. Previous anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation had often rested on the argument that stopping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would protect the life and health of pregnant women.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents had proposed 'heartbeat bills' in Texas, including in 2013 and 2019. But by 2021, the Supreme Court makeup was more favorable to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause, and dynamics in the state legislature had shifted.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Hughes\uc2 , the co-author of this year's new law, Senate Bill 8, used his new position as chair of the Senate Committee on State Affairs to pass several pieces of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation early in the session. And the House got a new speaker, Dade Phelan, who was willing to bring the bill forward for a vote, after the previous speaker assigned the 2019 version to languish in a committee chaired by a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Should the law survive the remaining legal challenges, the effective elimination of many more of the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics may raise demand for some services at nonprofit crisis pregnancy centers, like Prestonwood, which often offer baby supplies, parenting classes and other resources in addition to pregnancy tests and ultrasounds.\par \par \uc2 But critics of the centers say they can be deceptive, pointing out that they do not provide birth control or medical services that a pregnant woman needs to carry a baby to term and then thrive afterward. ''They congratulate themselves on having a diaper bank and car seats,'' said \uc2 Jeffrey Hons\uc2 , president and CEO of Planned Parenthood South Texas, which has temporarily suspended \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in response to the new law. ''People need health care.''\par \par \uc2 Protesters and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''sidewalk counselors'' are a constant presence outside Planned Parenthood South Texas. And a crisis pregnancy center is next door, sporting a similar blue and cream color scheme to Planned Parenthood in its signage. ''If you and your services could stand on their own, why do you have such a deceptive face to the public?'' Mr. Hons asked. ''I call that lying.''\par \par \uc2 Similarly, one of the two Prestonwood Pregnancy Centers in the Dallas area is across the street from a Planned Parenthood facility. But Leanne Jamieson, Prestonwood's executive director, said pregnancy centers like hers are needed in Texas, particularly now as residents grapple with the implications of the new law.\par \par \uc2 Over the last week, phones were ''ringing off the hook,'' she said. And in a signal of the optimism -- and opportunity -- the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement sees in this moment, Ms. Jamieson said she was considering expanding the center's prenatal medical care.\par \par \uc2 ''It might be we pivot in these times and look at: What are the needs of our community and what is it we are called to fill?'' she said. ''All the centers across the state are having that conversation.''\par \par \uc2 The Trust Respect Access Coalition hosted a ''Bans Off Our Bodies'' protest at the Texas Capitol on Sept. 1, the day the law took effect. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MONTINIQUE MONROE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Leanne Jamieson, executive director of Prestonwood Pregnancy Center, said she may expand prenatal care because of demand. (PHOTOGRAPH BY NITASHIA JOHNSON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A15) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210906eh960003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
