\par\par \uc2 MAUREEN DOWD\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 Drowning Our Future in the Past\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maureen Dowd \par \uc2 967 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- It isn't a pretty picture.\par \par \uc2 One coast is burning. The other is under water. In between, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  vigilantes may soon rampage across gunslinging territory.\par \par \uc2 What has happened to this country?\par \par \uc2 America is reeling backward, strangled by the past, nasty and uncaring, with everyone at one another's throats.\par \par \uc2 Post-Trump, we let ourselves hope that the new president could heal and soothe, restore a sense of rationality, decency and sanity. But the light at the end of the tunnel turned out to be just a firefly.\par \par \uc2 We feel the return of dread: We're rattled by the catastrophic exit from Afghanistan; the coming \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  war sparked by Texas; the Trumpian Supreme Court dragging us into the past; the confounding nature of this plague; the way Mother Nature is throttling us, leaving New Yorkers to drown in their basements. And now comes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , tromping toward another presidential run.\par \par \uc2 It feels as if nothing can be overcome. Everything is being relitigated.\par \par \uc2 We're choking on enlightened climate proposals but the disparity between the disasters we see, and what's being done in Washington, makes it feel as though nothing is happening except climate change. We're so far from getting a handle on the problem, the discussions around it seem almost theoretical.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin, tied to the energy industry, balks at climate change provisions in the reconciliation bill. He should be looking for ways to get West Virginia in touch with reality rather than living in the past.\par \par \uc2 ''Manchin's claim that climate pollution would be worsened by the elimination of fossil fuels -- or by the resolution's actual, more incremental climate provisions -- is highly dubious, if not outright false,'' The Intercept reported, noting that the truth is that Manchin's personal wealth would ''be impacted.'' Since he joined the Senate, The Intercept said, he has grossed some $4.5 million from coal companies he founded.\par \par \uc2 With its new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, sending women back to the back alley and encouraging Stasi-like participation from the citizenry, Texas now becomes the capital of American unreason. The law ''essentially delegated enforcement of that prohibition to the populace at large,'' wrote Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There were medieval fiefs more enlightened than the Lone Star G.O.P.\par \par \uc2 Between putting women in danger by pushing that law and putting children in danger by imposing his anti-mask mania on school districts that want to mask up, Gov. Greg Abbott of Texas has become a scourge of the first rank.\par \par \uc2 A cynical slice of the Republican Party -- and this includes Trump -- privately denigrates anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists as wackos, but publicly moves in lock-step with them in order to cling to that base and keep power.\par \par \uc2 But the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces were somehow clever enough to hijack the Supreme Court and Republicans will have to contend with the backlash when the court tosses Roe v. Wade aside.\par \par \uc2 As botched as the withdrawal from Afghanistan was, at least \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was trying to move into the future and do triage on one of America's worst mistakes.\par \par \uc2 And unlike other presidents -- J.F.K. with the Bay of Pigs, L.B.J. with the Vietnam War and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  with the Afghanistan surge -- Biden did not allow himself to be suckered by the generals, the overweening Ivy Leaguers and the Blob, the expense account monsters who keep this town whirring and always have a seat at the table, no matter how wrong they were, and are.\par \par \uc2 The Afghanistan tragedy, as James Risen wrote in The Intercept, was just two decades of Americans lying to one another, and it ''brought out in Americans the same imperial arrogance that doomed the U.S. involvement in Vietnam.''\par \par \uc2 Unlike his three predecessors, Biden risked Saudi ire by directing the Justice Department and other agencies on Friday to review and declassify documents related to the F.B.I.'s investigation into 9/11. Families of 9/11 victims had been pushing for the release of the secret files to learn more about the role the Saudis played in the attacks.\par \par \uc2 The enablers of our misbegotten occupations of Afghanistan and Iraq have been shrieking like banshees at Biden, trying to manacle him to their own past mistakes as he attempts to lift off.\par \par \uc2 With peerless chutzpah, \uc2 Tony Blair\uc2  called Biden's decision to depart cynical and driven by an ''imbecilic political slogan about ending 'the forever wars.'''\par \par \uc2 But Biden knew enough not to spend more lives and treasure to prop up a kleptocracy. He oversaw some bad weeks in Afghanistan but \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Rumsfeld\uc2  should be blamed for 20 bad years.\par \par \uc2 Remarkably, as Jon Allsop pointed out in The Columbia Journalism Review, the word ''Bush'' was not mentioned once on any of the Sunday news shows the weekend Kabul was falling.\par \par \uc2 ''He looks like the Babe Ruth of presidents when you compare him to Trump,'' Harry Reid, the former Democratic Senate majority leader, told The Washington Post's Ben Terris, for a story this past week on Bush nostalgia.\par \par \uc2 With a memory like a goldfish, America circles its bowl, returning to where we have been, unable to move forward, condemned to repeat a past we should escape.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 A flooded expressway in Brooklyn on Thursday after record rainfall drenched New York.   (PHOTOGRAPH BY Ed Jones/Agence France-Presse -- Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210905eh950004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
