\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 No Comment Is Top Reply To New Law Roiling Texas\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Gelles \par \uc2 1,472 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Businesses that expressed opposition to restrictive voting laws are declining to take a similar stand on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure.\par \par \uc2 When Texas lawmakers advanced a restrictive voting rights bill this year, \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  and \uc2 Dell Technologies\uc2 , two of the state's biggest employers, were early and vocal critics of the effort.\par \par \uc2 But this week, as a law that prohibits most abortions after about six weeks took effect in Texas, both companies declined to comment on the measure.\par \par \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  and \uc2 Dell\uc2  were representative of the business community at large. While many corporations are taking stands on voting rights, climate change, immigration and other important matters, few companies would comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one of the country's most politically and emotionally charged issues, and as much as 40 percent of the American public supports outlawing or severely restricting it, according to the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 . Opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  often cuts across demographic groups, and most executives would be reluctant to take a public stand that was likely to anger or alienate a large group of customers and employees whatever they said.\par \par \uc2 ''No one is going to walk willingly through this door,'' said Sandra Sucher, a professor of management at Harvard Business School. ''If I'm a business making a political calculus, it's just a matter of who I'm going to piss off.''\par \par \uc2 Two dozen major companies contacted by The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  on Friday either did not reply or declined to comment. Among those that would not say something were \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , a sponsor of International Women's Day; PwC, a major supporter of diversity and inclusion efforts; and \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  and \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2 , which led a corporate backlash last year against a restrictive voting bill in Georgia, where they have their headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Many of the biggest employers in Texas, including \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , \uc2 Oracle\uc2 , \uc2 McKesson\uc2  and \uc2 Phillips 66\uc2 , declined to comment. Even companies that are quick to speak up on social issues, including Patagonia and Levi's, did not say anything about the new law. And Catalyst, a nonprofit organization that teams up with big companies to ''build workplaces that work for women,'' declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 ''When all of these companies who participate in things like International Women's Day won't speak out on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, it shows that they care about the bottom line, not what women need and want,'' said Lindsey Taylor Wood, chief executive of The Helm, a venture capital firm that funds female founders.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Elizabeth Graham\uc2 , a vice president of Texas Right to Life, a group that backs the law, said it would be good for business in the state, claiming that a majority of people there are ''conservative and pro-life.''\par \par \uc2 ''Many of our supporters are small and medium-sized business owners,'' she said. ''They are very much in favor of it.''\par \par \uc2 Joe Pojman, executive director for Texas Alliance for Life, another group that supports the new law, said he had seen scant evidence of any pushback from the businesses.\par \par \uc2 ''We just haven't seen any evidence of that, and we frankly are grateful for that,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Before the law, known as Senate Bill 8, went into effect on Wednesday, some legal experts had argued it would face legal challenges that would postpone its enforcement or ultimately strike it down. The law empowers private citizens to sue anyone who performs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or ''aids and abets'' such a procedure, a broad definition that could include a driver for a ride-hailing company who takes a woman to a health clinic.\par \par \uc2 But the Supreme Court declined on Wednesday night to block the law, which rules out \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an option before most women even know they are pregnant in the second-most-populous state, while the legal challenge to it continues in court.\par \par \uc2 ''Companies were caught off guard,'' said Jen Stark, an executive at the Tara Health Foundation, which has organized companies in support of reproductive issues. ''Usually the courts have stepped in.''\par \par \uc2 Over the past few days, companies have been scrambling to decide what, if anything, they would say about the new law.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an issue that is closely associated with religious views, an area where businesses are exceedingly cautious.\par \par \uc2 ''Keeping religion out of business has been one of the ways that companies try to create a safe space for everyone,'' Professor Sucher said. ''This marches squarely into the realm of religious views.''\par \par \uc2 The governor of Texas, Greg Abbott, who championed the law, has said it would not hurt the state economically, including its longstanding efforts to get businesses to move there from higher-cost and more liberal parts of the country like California and New York. He argued that some employers would be drawn to the state because of its conservative laws, citing \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , who runs Tesla and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , as one such executive.\par \par \uc2 ''This is not slowing down businesses coming to the state of Texas at all,'' Mr. Abbott, a Republican, told CNBC on Thursday. ''In fact, it is accelerating the process of businesses coming to Texas.''\par \par \uc2 But while most executives have stayed quiet, a few have spoken out.\par \par \uc2 Bumble, the dating app company founded by \uc2 Whitney Wolfe Herd\uc2  and based in Austin, said it was donating funds to organizations that supported women in Texas seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''Bumble is woman-founded and women-led, and from day one we've stood up for the most vulnerable,'' the company said in an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  post. ''We'll keep fighting against regressive laws like #SB8.''\par \par \uc2 Match Group\uc2 , another dating company, which is based in Dallas, also set up a fund for employees affected by the law, and \uc2 Shar Dubey\uc2 , the chief executive, sent a memo to employees expressing her disappointment in the new law.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, some Silicon Valley technology companies began speaking out, too.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's chief executive, \uc2 Logan Green\uc2 , said the company would pay the legal costs of any drivers who faced lawsuits under the law. ''TX SB8 threatens to punish drivers for getting people where they need to go -- especially women exercising their right to choose,'' he wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Uber's chief executive, \uc2 Dara Khosrowshahi\uc2 , said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that his company would also cover its drivers' legal expenses.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Jeremy Stoppelman\uc2 , the chief executive of Yelp, issued a statement. ''The effective ban on abortions in Texas not only infringes on women's rights to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, but it puts their health and safety at greater risk,'' he said. ''We are deeply concerned about how this law will impact our employees in the state.''\par \par \uc2 A couple executives tried to find a middle ground, cheering on democracy and opposing discrimination while remaining silent on the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk, who said he has moved to Texas and was investing a lot in the state through Tesla and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , was among them. ''In general, I believe government should rarely impose its will upon the people, and, when doing so, should aspire to maximize their cumulative happiness,'' he wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  in response to Mr. Abbott's comments. ''That said, I would prefer to stay out of politics.''\par \par \uc2 Hewlett Packard Enterprise\uc2 , based in Houston, declined to comment on the ban, but said the company ''encourages our team members to engage in the political process where they live and work and make their voices heard through advocacy and at the voting booth.''\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the company added that its medical plan allowed employees to seek abortions out of state, and would pay for lodging for such a trip.\par \par \uc2 By Friday afternoon, at least two nascent efforts to organize a broader corporate response opposing the law were underway. It was unclear what would come of the conversations, as many companies involved in the discussions were wary about inserting themselves in the debate.\par \par \uc2 ''Someone wants to be first and no one wants to be first,'' Ms. Stark, the Tara Health Foundation executive, said.\par \par \uc2 Even before the Texas law was passed, corporations were cautious about addressing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 A 2019 effort, Don't Ban Equality, called for companies to offer wide access to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it attracted only a handful of large employers.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, many of the companies that had signed on to that effort, including Bloomberg, Glossier, Slack and \uc2 Postmates\uc2 , did not reply to requests for comment on the new Texas law.\par \par \uc2 And two years ago, when lawmakers in Georgia advanced a restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, movie companies that invested heavily in the state largely stayed on the sidelines of the debate about the measure, even as some actors spoke out.\par \par \uc2 A ''Bans Off Our Bodies'' protest at the Texas Capitol in Austin this week. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Montinique Monroe for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210904eh940003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
