\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Activists Target Anti- \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Group's Web Presence\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicole Perlroth \par \uc2 1,033 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 To protest Texas' new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, activists said, they pranked a website set up by the state's largest anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group.\par \par \uc2 After a Texas law restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  went into effect on Wednesday, the state's largest anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group publicized a website that invited citizens to inform on the law's violators.\par \par \uc2 The website, prolifewhistleblower.com, which was set up by the group Texas Right to Life, was designed to help carry out the new law. That's because the law places enforcement not in the hands of state officials but with private citizens, who are deputized to sue anyone who performs or aids an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in violation of the law.\par \par \uc2 Tips about the law's potential offenders quickly flooded into the website, which features an online form so people can anonymously submit reports of those who are illegally obtaining or facilitating abortions.\par \par \uc2 But some of the tips were a little unexpected.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Greg Abbott of Texas, who was a leading proponent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, was a violator, according to some of the tips. The fictional characters from Marvel's Avengers were also apparently seeking abortions, the reports said. Other tips did not point to individuals but instead contained copies of the entire script to the 2007 animated film ''Bee Movie.''\par \par \uc2 The reports, which were obviously bogus, were the work of activists on TikTok, programmers, and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and Reddit users who said they wanted to ensnarl the site's administrators in fabricated data.\par \par \uc2 Their digital dissent was part of a wave of reaction against the Texas law, which bans most abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy and makes the state the most restrictive in the nation in terms of access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. President Biden said Thursday that the situation ''unleashes unconstitutional chaos'' against women.\par \par \uc2 On TikTok, a developer with the alias Sean Black said he had developed a script that automatically generated fake reports to the prolifewhistleblower.com site. After the site tried to block him, the developer released a shortcut that allowed anyone with an iPhone or iPad to automatically create a fake report using a randomly generated Texas ZIP code.\par \par \uc2 ''What if somebody very technical, very handsome, set up a bot that automatically sent the request to their website,'' the developer said on TikTok. ''Oh wait. It was me. I did that.''\par \par \uc2 The developer, who declined to provide his real name for fear of retaliation, said in an interview that more than 7,200 people had clicked on his script and that more than 8,450 people had clicked on the shortcut as of Thursday afternoon, based on data from Linktree, a service that helps people manage their website content. The shortcut was reported earlier by Vice.\par \par \uc2 On \uc2 GitHub\uc2 , a website for sharing and collaborating on software code, another programmer, Jonathan D\u237\'00\'EDaz, released a script and posted a link on Thursday to a new app, Pro-Life Buster, which allowed people to automatically spam the Texas website with ''bogus tips.'' The developer wrote that the script was a way to push back against the law because it was ''no one's business to know about people's abortions.''\par \par \uc2 By Thursday evening, the app showed that 1,000 new reports had been shared.\par \par \uc2 Mr. D\u237\'00\'EDaz said the app existed to flood the site with authentic-looking, but fabricated, data. ''The goal is to waste these people's time and resources so that they wake up and realize this effort is not worth their time,'' he said Friday.\par \par \uc2 These techniques, known as ''hacktivism,'' have become increasingly prevalent. Last year, TikTok teens and fans of Korean pop music inundated a rally website for former President Donald J. Trump with fake registrations -- and then never showed up, leaving thousands of seats conspicuously empty. Anonymous, the loose hacking collective, has protested policies from the Vatican, the C.I.A. and others by flooding their websites with junk traffic to try to force them offline.\par \par \uc2 Kim Schwartz, a spokeswoman for Texas Right to Life, denied that the group's website had been overwhelmed with false reports. ''We knew this would happen, and we were prepared,'' she said. ''Activists have been trying to spam and take down the site for a week and failed.''\par \par \uc2 Even so, the group's website appeared to periodically buckle on Thursday and drop under the load of reports, according to screenshots posted to Reddit and other sites. Separately late Friday, a judge in Travis County, Texas, granted a temporary restraining order against Texas Right to Life, blocking it from suing Planned Parenthood and enforcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 To stem the flood of automated reports to its website, Texas Right to Life's administrators have added a new version of a Captcha, a program that tries to filter real human responses from automated computer reports.\par \par \uc2 But some hacktivists persisted. One posted a screenshot on Reddit of a fake report that pointed to some of Marvel's Avengers as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers. On \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , people posted screenshots of other fake tips. One user sarcastically reported that he wanted to retroactively abort his 30-year-old son who apparently wouldn't leave the house.\par \par \uc2 Others on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  called for a boycott of GoDaddy, the company that hosts the Texas Right to Life tip site. They claimed the site violated GoDaddy's rules that prohibit customers from collecting or harvesting nonpublic information about anyone without their ''prior written consent.''\par \par \uc2 GoDaddy said late Thursday that it had given Texas Right to Life 24 hours to find a new hosting provider before cutting off service.\par \par \uc2 ''We have informed prolifewhistleblower.com they have 24 hours to move to another provider for violating our terms of service,'' Dan C. Race, a GoDaddy spokesman, said in an email.\par \par \uc2 By Friday afternoon, some people were having trouble submitting tips to the website using the form. Others reported seeing a GoDaddy firewall page instead of the profilewhistleblower.com site.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, protests against the new \uc2  law extended online, with efforts to snarl traffic to a website that invited citizens to inform on the law's violators. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MONTINIQUE MONROE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210904eh940003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
