\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Conservatives Spent Decades Trying to Achieve This Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carl Hulse \par \uc2 1,409 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Forces on the right have spent decades maneuvering to install a reliable anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  majority. Now they have one, and it is paying off for them.\par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court declined late Wednesday to block a severely restrictive Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, it was fulfilling the long-held ambitions of a series of committed Republican presidents, senators and conservative activists who worked unceasingly for years to cement a reliable anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  majority on the court.\par \par \uc2 The decision confirmed the worst fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, who had long warned that conservatives were moving aggressively to put in place a court majority that would upend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. And it showed the success of a carefully orchestrated master plan that required deep coordination among the conservative legal community, the White House and the Senate, combined with the willingness of Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  of Kentucky to play confirmation hardball.\par \par \uc2 Mr. McConnell, the court-focused Republican leader, denied one Democratic president the right to fill one Supreme Court seat and then raced to fill another with a G.O.P. nominee before a subsequent Democratic president could. Both decisions transformed the ideological makeup of the court and made this week's decision possible, with the court facing an opportunity to act more definitively against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights this fall.\par \par \uc2 ''This has been the crux of our political strategy for decades,'' said Mallory Quigley, the vice president for communications at the conservative \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  List. ''It has been to elect pro-life presidents, pro-life senators and put in these pro-life legislators so they could nominate and confirm pro-life Supreme Court justices.''\par \par \uc2 Those same justices all declared Roe v. Wade well-established precedent and the law of the land during their hearings before the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , in a time-honored tradition of offering vague euphemisms meant to convey judicial neutrality during their confirmation periods. They promised to keep open minds, testifying that no one involved in their nomination had even asked for their view of the case.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats were always skeptical, pointing to the conservative backgrounds of the nominees and their strong support from the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , a conservative pipeline for federal judges. Opponents now say their deep misgivings are being borne out.\par \par \uc2 ''We warned about it during the confirmation hearings,'' said Senator Richard Blumenthal, a Connecticut Democrat who sits on the Judiciary Committee. ''We refused to believe the lies from Supreme Court nominees that they would adhere to precedent.''\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, it was the vindication of a bet made years ago that a focus on the court could help them achieve their policy objectives even if they did not have broad support among the public.\par \par \uc2 The long-range conservative game plan reached fruition during the administration of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , who won election in part by promising to put a conservative in the court seat Mr. McConnell left dangling open. Enthusiastically assisted by Mr. McConnell and Senate Republicans, Mr. Trump ended up naming three justices seen as hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Those three, \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett, joined with Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . in declining to block the Texas law widely denounced as violating Supreme Court precedent, putting the court on the precipice of overriding Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 While the ruling was a win for conservatives, it posed political risks for Republicans and could stir a backlash that energizes Democrats and female voters before the 2022 midterm elections, which will be held after an anticipated court ruling on a second restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 President Biden assailed the decision as ''an unprecedented assault on a woman's constitutional rights'' and promised to initiate a governmentwide review to find ways to maintain access to abortions.\par \par \uc2 Democratic lawmakers called for Congress to enact legislation codifying guarantees to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , labeling the ruling the act of a ''radically partisan court,'' said the House would vote on such a plan when it returns this month, even though Republicans would almost surely filibuster such a measure in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The decision led to renewed calls to expand the Supreme Court to offset the conservative majority that Democrats and progressives say was wrongly installed when Mr. McConnell refused to allow President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  to fill the seat of \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  after he died in 2016. The Republican leader then raced to seat Justice Barrett just days before Mr. Biden was elected last year. Mr. Biden has created a commission to study ways to overhaul the court, but has not endorsed a change.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the movement say that they still have a ways to go in building the necessary momentum to add seats to the court, but that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision will aid their efforts as Americans see conservatives and the court realizing their goal of greatly limiting access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''This is what they advertised they were going to do, and now they are doing it,'' Brian Fallon, the executive director of the progressive group Demand Justice, said about the court. ''Now the only question is what are Democrats going to do about it?''\par \par \uc2 The decision will also intensify calls for broader change in the Senate such as weakening or eliminating the filibuster. Any move to enlarge the court or make other substantial changes to it would most likely require barring the filibuster, as would enacting new voting rights laws that activists say are needed to counter the impact of the conservative court.\par \par \uc2 Mr. McConnell, recognizing the complex politics surrounding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was reserved in his reaction to the decision despite his hand in reshaping the court.\par \par \uc2 ''I think it was a highly technical decision,'' he told reporters at an event in Kentucky on Thursday. ''Whether it leads to a broader ruling on Roe vs. Wade is unclear at this point.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. McConnell's role in constituting the current court can hardly be overstated. After Justice Scalia's death in February 2016, Mr. McConnell rallied Senate Republicans to block the nomination of \uc2 Merrick B. Garland\uc2  to the seat, saying that openings should not be filled in presidential election years.\par \par \uc2 The vacancy gave Mr. Trump the chance to attract the support of conservatives wary of him as he openly promised to appoint conservatives he expected to overturn Roe v. Wade, saying it would happen ''automatically'' when his nominees were seated. Mr. Trump took the unique step of producing a list of potential nominees to further assure conservatives.\par \par \uc2 After Mr. Trump's election, he, Mr. McConnell and \uc2 Donald F. McGahn II\uc2 , then the White House counsel, aggressively set out to put a heavily conservative imprint on both the Supreme Court and the lower courts.\par \par \uc2 They started with Justice Gorsuch, who was placed on the court weeks after Mr. Trump took office and Republicans changed Senate rules to prevent Democrats from blocking the nominee through a filibuster. He was followed in 2018 by Justice Kavanaugh, the replacement for Justice \uc2 Anthony M. Kennedy\uc2 , who voted repeatedly to uphold \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Then, late in 2020 came Justice Barrett, nominated by Mr. Trump to replace Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights champion. Despite his protests four years earlier that Supreme Court justices should not be confirmed in presidential election years, Mr. McConnell said that stance applied only when presidents and Senate majorities were of differing parties, as they were in 2016. He hurriedly shepherded Justice Barrett through and she was confirmed in late October, denying Mr. Biden the chance to choose Justice Ginsburg's replacement.\par \par \uc2 The confirmation of Justice Barrett was particularly critical since she provided a sixth conservative vote and was considered a hedge against Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 ., who has come to be seen by some conservatives as insufficiently committed to their causes. The chief justice broke with his fellow Republican appointees in dissenting from the Texas decision.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Blumenthal suggested that while the conservative game plan was succeeding, Republicans might come to regret that it is working as well as it has.\par \par \uc2 ''It is the culmination of years of calculated strategy and manipulation, but it is really explosive for the court,'' he said. ''The court just sort of lit the house on fire.''\par \par \uc2 While the ruling was a win for conservatives, it poses political risks for Republicans and could stir a backlash that energizes Democrats. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Sarahbeth Maney/The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210903eh930003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
