\par\par \uc2 LAUREN KELLEY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Is This How Roe v. Wade Dies?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Kelley \par \uc2 985 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In May, the Supreme Court agreed to take its first major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case since the death of Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  -- a direct challenge to Roe v. Wade, out of Mississippi. That news induced nausea in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates around the country, as they waited to see what the court's newly turbocharged conservative majority would do after oral arguments in the case expected to be heard this fall.\par \par \uc2 Three months later, it would be almost a relief if that case, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, were the most immediate threat to American \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On Tuesday night, the Supreme Court got away with something much more insidious: It all but ended \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Texas, at least for now. It simultaneously dealt a blow to Roe v. Wade that could hasten its demise -- by saying nothing and thus quietly allowing a state anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, now the most restrictive one in the nation, to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 The law, SB8, bans abortions after six weeks of pregnancy -- so early that many people don't know they're pregnant, and before some doctors will even provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Several states have already passed six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, sometimes called heartbeat bills, and other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions with very early limits. A wave of such laws swept through several states in 2019, prompting calls for boycotts against doing business in those states. None of those laws are being enforced today because they're obviously unconstitutional: Roe v. Wade guarantees the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the point of fetal viability, or about 22 weeks of pregnancy. So while the laws created a splash, they didn't affect many people's lives. Until this week.\par \par \uc2 But SB8 is much more diabolical than the average six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. The law would allow just about anyone -- truly, almost any person, anywhere -- to sue people or entities who ''abet'' or even allegedly intend to ''abet'' abortions in Texas after six weeks of pregnancy. What does it mean to ''abet'' an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? The law isn't entirely clear on that. But it's easy to think of ways it could be interpreted: a friend gives a woman money for a procedure, a taxi driver drops someone off at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, a receptionist is stationed inside the front door. Plaintiffs who win their cases would get at least $10,000 each -- a provision that seems destined to create a state full of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounty hunters.\par \par \uc2 What, as they say, could go wrong?\par \par \uc2 The law was also written in such a way that it's very difficult to fight in court. Because SB8 is supposed to be enforced by everyday citizens, there's no single Texas official whom \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates can sue to block the law, as they typically would in such cases. This issue was supposed to be heard last week by a Federal District Court, which could have issued an injunction blocking the law. But in a move that baffled many legal experts, a panel of judges on the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit canceled that hearing at the last minute, paving the way for the law to go into effect this week.\par \par \uc2 The only option \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates had left was to go to the Supreme Court -- the court that's now dominated by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  justices, including three appointed by Donald Trump specifically to end legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America. So the advocates did just that, and then waited as the clock ticked past midnight Central time, with no word from the court to stop the law from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 What happens now? The Texas law could still be temporarily blocked by the Supreme Court. But for now \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state is in chaos. On Tuesday night, Whole Woman's Health, which operates \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Texas and elsewhere, tweeted that its Texas waiting rooms were full with patients desperate to get abortions in the hours and minutes before the law went into effect.\par \par \uc2 It now seems likely that more laws like SB8 will pass, as other anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  state leaders will surely try to follow Texas' lead. Why wouldn't they? The Supreme Court may not yet have ruled on the merits of Texas' law, as some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners would no doubt prefer, but the state's wild ploy was clearly successful in threatening the future of clinics across the state. In that way, the court gave a green light to lawmakers everywhere who have been itching for decades to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 As for the people of Texas, many of them will get abortions despite SB8, either by crossing borders or by purchasing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills on the internet. This is a certainty. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes have long claimed that their aim isn't to punish individual women, who they say are merely victims of dastardly \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. But it is clear what happens in countries where abortions are outlawed: Women end up in jail.\par \par \uc2 As the court's conservatives have chipped away at \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent decades, the central fear among supporters of those rights was that an emboldened hard-right majority would eventually overturn Roe v. Wade. What became painfully clear after Tuesday night is that it may not have to. Without even lifting a finger, the court showed what may well be America's near future: a country in which Roe v. Wade is not only hobbled, but a dead letter altogether.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY TOM BRENNER/REUTERS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210902eh920003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
