\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justices' Slience Lets Texas Halt Most Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak, J. David Goodman and Sabrina Tavernise \par \uc2 2,111 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The law, which prohibits most abortions after six weeks and went into effect on Wednesday, was drafted by Texas lawmakers with the goal of frustrating efforts to challenge it in federal court.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court refused just before midnight on Wednesday to block a Texas law prohibiting most abortions, less than a day after it took effect and became the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure in the nation.\par \par \uc2 The vote was 5 to 4, with Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . joining the court's three liberal members in dissent.\par \par \uc2 The majority opinion was unsigned and consisted of a single long paragraph. It said the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who had challenged the law in an emergency application to the court had not made their case in the face of ''complex and novel'' procedural questions. The majority stressed that it was not ruling on the constitutionality of the Texas law and did not mean to limit ''procedurally proper challenges'' to it.\par \par \uc2 But the ruling was certain to fuel the hopes of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents and fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates as the court takes up a separate case in its new term this fall to decide whether Roe v. Wade, the landmark 1973 decision establishing a constitutional right to the procedure, should be overruled. It also left Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers turning away patients as they scrambled to comply with the law, which prohibits abortions after roughly six weeks.\par \par \uc2 All four dissenting justices filed opinions.\par \par \uc2 ''The court's order is stunning,'' Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  wrote in her dissent. ''Presented with an application to enjoin a flagrantly unconstitutional law engineered to prohibit women from exercising their constitutional rights and evade judicial scrutiny, a majority of justices have opted to bury their heads in the sand.''\par \par \uc2 ''The court has rewarded the state's effort to delay federal review of a plainly unconstitutional statute, enacted in disregard of the court's precedents, through procedural entanglements of the state's own creation,'' Justice Sotomayor wrote. ''The court should not be so content to ignore its constitutional obligations to protect not only the rights of women, but also the sanctity of its precedents and of the rule of law.''\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Roberts wrote that he would have blocked the law while appeals moved forward.\par \par \uc2 ''The statutory scheme before the court is not only unusual, but unprecedented,'' he wrote. ''The legislature has imposed a prohibition on abortions after roughly six weeks, and then essentially delegated enforcement of that prohibition to the populace at large. The desired consequence appears to be to insulate the state from responsibility for implementing and enforcing the regulatory regime.''\par \par \uc2 The chief justice underscored the tentative nature of the majority's ruling. ''Although the court denies the applicants' request for emergency relief today,'' he wrote, ''the court's order is emphatic in making clear that it cannot be understood as sustaining the constitutionality of the law at issue.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Elena Kagan criticized the court's practice of deciding important issues in rushed decisions without full briefing or oral argument -- on what Supreme Court specialists call its ''shadow docket.''\par \par \uc2 ''Today's ruling illustrates just how far the court's 'shadow-docket' decisions may depart from the usual principles of appellate process,'' she wrote. ''That ruling, as everyone must agree, is of great consequence.''\par \par \uc2 ''Yet the majority has acted without any guidance from the court of appeals -- which is right now considering the same issues,'' she wrote. ''It has reviewed only the most cursory party submissions, and then only hastily. And it barely bothers to explain its conclusion -- that a challenge to an obviously unconstitutional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation backed by a wholly unprecedented enforcement scheme is unlikely to prevail.''\par \par \uc2 ''In all these ways,'' Justice Kagan wrote, ''the majority's decision is emblematic of too much of this court's shadow-docket decision making -- which every day becomes more unreasoned, inconsistent and impossible to defend.''\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, known as Senate Bill 8, amounts to a nearly complete ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas because 85 to 90 percent of procedures in the state happen after the sixth week of pregnancy, according to lawyers for several clinics. On Tuesday night, clinics were scrambling to see patients until the minute the law went into effect, with six-hour waits for procedures in some places. By Wednesday, the patient lists had shrunk, clinic workers said in interviews.\par \par \uc2 The law is the latest battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the United States. In recent years, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners have found success through laws in state legislatures, and a broad swath of the South and the Midwest now has limited access to abortions.\par \par \uc2 Texas has about 24 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, down from roughly 40 before 2013, when the State Legislature imposed a previous round of restrictions. It was not immediately clear on Wednesday if every one of them was complying with the law, which the Republican governor signed in May, but many, in interviews, said they were.\par \par \uc2 In the emergency application urging the justices to intervene, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the state said the new law ''would immediately and catastrophically reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Texas,'' and most likely force ''many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics ultimately to close.''\par \par \uc2 Supreme Court precedents prohibit states from banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability, the point at which fetuses can sustain life outside the womb, or about 22 to 24 weeks into a pregnancy. The law in Texas says doctors cannot perform abortions if a heartbeat is detected, activity that starts at around six weeks, before many women are even aware they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Many states have passed such bans, but the law in Texas is different. It was drafted to make it difficult to challenge in court.\par \par \uc2 Usually, a lawsuit seeking to block a law because it is unconstitutional would name state officials as defendants. However, the Texas law, which makes no exceptions for pregnancies resulting from incest or rape, bars state officials from enforcing it and instead deputizes private individuals to sue anyone who performs the procedure or ''aids and abets'' it.\par \par \uc2 The patient may not be sued, but doctors, staff members at clinics, counselors, people who help pay for the procedure, and even an Uber driver taking a patient to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic are all potential defendants. Plaintiffs, who do not need to live in Texas, have any connection to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or show any injury from it, are entitled to $10,000 and their legal fees recovered if they win. Prevailing defendants are not entitled to legal fees.\par \par \uc2 That novel formulation has sent clinics scrambling.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jessica Rubino, a doctor at Austin Women's Health Center, a small, independent clinic in the state capital, said that at first, she wanted to defy what appeared to be an unconstitutional law. But she said she concluded that doing so would put her staff at risk.\par \par \uc2 ''If this was a criminal ban, we'd know what this is and what we can and cannot do,'' Dr. Rubino said. ''But this ban has civil implications. It requires a lawyer to go to court. It requires lawyers' fees. And then $10,000 if we don't win. What happens if everybody is sued, not just me?''\par \par \uc2 She added: ''My staff is nervous. They've been asking, 'What about our families?'''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Rubino said her clinic had ''struggled so much to come up with any plan to take care of anyone'' under the new law, and on Wednesday was sorting out what the new policies would be. For example, she wondered, if someone knows they are more than six or seven weeks pregnant -- roughly the new legal limit -- should the clinic advise them to go out of state and not waste money on an ultrasound?\par \par \uc2 Doctors who are sued, even if the suit is dismissed, have to report the lawsuits when they renew licenses or obtain hospital admitting privileges, according to Amy Hagstrom Miller, the chief executive at Whole Woman's Health, which operates four clinics in Texas.\par \par \uc2 There was little indication of the shifting legal ground outside the Planned Parenthood Center for Choice in Houston, the group's only location in the city that provides \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. A blue bus offering free pregnancy tests from an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, a regular presence, sat across the street. But inside, the effect was clear: Dr. Bhavik Kumar, a staff physician, said he had seen six patients by Wednesday afternoon, down from his usual 30.\par \par \uc2 At Whole Woman's Health of Fort Worth, the last patient appointment was completed at 11:56 p.m. on Tuesday, said Marva Sadler, the organization's senior director of clinic services. She said doctors started early on Tuesday morning and treated 117 patients, far more than usual.\par \par \uc2 ''It was absolutely organized chaos,'' said Ms. Sadler, who had come from San Antonio to help out. ''Patients were waiting upward of five and six hours to have their procedures done.''\par \par \uc2 She said patients were waiting in their cars, and also in the waiting room. Some were told to come back later. On Wednesday, she said, the clinic was in uncharted waters. Of the 79 people on the schedule, she estimated that about 20 would be able to eventually complete their procedures. Many, she said, would be too far along in their pregnancies to be treated under the new law.\par \par \uc2 ''People are confused,'' she said. ''They don't know where to go. They don't know what this law is.''\par \par \uc2 The immediate question for the justices was not whether the Texas law is constitutional, but whether it may be challenged in federal court. The law's defenders say that, given the way the law is structured, only Texas courts can rule on the matter and only in the context of suits against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers for violating the law.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's ruling was provisional. The challenge to the law remains pending in the lower federal courts, and they are poised to sort through the complex issues in the case.\par \par \uc2 As the law came into force, Democrats assailed it and pledged to fight to retain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Texas and nationwide. In a statement, President Biden said the measure ''blatantly violates'' the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  established by Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 In its next term, which starts in October, the Supreme Court is set to decide whether Roe v. Wade should be overruled in a case from Mississippi concerning a state law banning most abortions after 15 weeks that has been blocked by the courts.\par \par \uc2 The Texas case, which was on the court's ''shadow docket'' without a full briefing or oral arguments, leapfrogged the one from Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 The Texas and Mississippi laws are among many measures enacted by Republican-controlled state legislatures intended to test the durability of Roe and Planned Parenthood v. Casey, the 1992 decision that affirmed Roe's core holding and said states may not impose an ''undue burden'' on the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 The lawmakers behind the various state-based measures are betting that the Supreme Court's recent shift to the right will lead it to sustain the new laws. The court now includes three members appointed by President Donald J. Trump, who had vowed to name justices prepared to overrule Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 One of them, Justice \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2 , replaced Justice \uc2 Anthony M. Kennedy\uc2 , a cautious supporter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Another, Justice Amy Coney Barrett, replaced Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , who viewed access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as essential to women's autonomy and equality.\par \par \uc2 Two months after Senate Bill 8 was signed into law by Gov. Greg Abbott, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas filed suit in federal court, naming, among others, every state trial court judge and county court clerk in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The defendants responded that they were not proper parties and were, in any event, immune from being sued.\par \par \uc2 A federal trial judge rejected a motion to dismiss the case and scheduled a hearing on whether to block the law. But the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit, in New Orleans, canceled the hearing.\par \par \uc2 The challengers said they were at minimum entitled to a decision on their request for the law to be temporarily suspended.\par \par \uc2 At Whole Woman's Health, which operates four clinics in Texas, including in Austin, above left, Tuesday was chaotic as women rushed to be treated before the law took effect. Despite its silence, the Supreme Court, above right, could still decide to block the strict law. A protest, below, on Wednesday at the State Capitol in Austin. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MONTINIQUE MONROE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; SARAHBETH MANEY/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A16) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210902eh920003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
