\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Texas' Near-Ban on Abortions Takes Effect After Supreme Court Fails to Act\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 638 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The court is still expected to rule soon on an emergency application from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers seeking to block the measure, which is now the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the nation.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Supreme Court did not take action early Wednesday on a request to block a Texas law prohibiting most abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy, allowing the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the nation to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 The law, known as Senate Bill 8, amounts to a nearly complete ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas, one that will further fuel legal and political battles over the future of Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The law makes no exceptions for pregnancies resulting from incest or rape.\par \par \uc2 An emergency application from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers seeking to block the law remains pending, and the court is expected to rule on it shortly.\par \par \uc2 In the application, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers wrote that the law ''would immediately and catastrophically reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Texas, barring care for at least 85 percent of Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients (those who are six weeks pregnant or greater) and likely forcing many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics ultimately to close.''\par \par \uc2 Supreme Court precedents forbid states from banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability, the point at which fetuses can sustain life outside the womb, or about 22 to 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 But the Texas law was drafted to make it difficult to challenge in court. Usually, a lawsuit seeking to block a law because it is unconstitutional would name state officials as defendants. But the Texas law bars state officials from enforcing it and instead deputizes private individuals to sue anyone who performs the procedure or ''aids and abets'' it.\par \par \uc2 The patient may not be sued, but doctors, staff members at clinics, counselors, people who help pay for the procedure, even an Uber driver taking a patient to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic are all potential defendants. Plaintiffs, who need not have any connection to the matter or show any injury from it, are entitled to $10,000 and their legal fees recovered if they win. Prevailing defendants are not entitled to legal fees.\par \par \uc2 In its next term, which starts in October, the Supreme Court is already set to decide whether Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , should be overruled in a case from Mississippi concerning a state law banning abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The Texas and Mississippi laws are among many measures enacted by Republican-controlled state legislatures intended to test the durability of Roe and Planned Parenthood v. Casey, the 1992 decision that affirmed Roe's core holding and said states may not impose an ''undue burden'' on the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 The lawmakers behind the various state-based measures are betting that the Supreme Court's recent shift to the right will lead it to sustain the new laws. The court now includes three members appointed by President Donald J. Trump, who had vowed to name justices prepared to overrule Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 One of them, Justice \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2 , replaced Justice \uc2 Anthony M. Kennedy\uc2 , a cautious supporter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Another, Justice Amy Coney Barrett, replaced Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , who viewed access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as essential to women's autonomy and equality.\par \par \uc2 Senate Bill 8 was signed into law in May by Gov. Greg Abbott, a Republican. It prohibits doctors from performing abortions if a fetal heartbeat is detected.\par \par \uc2 Such activity starts at around six weeks, before many women are even aware that they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2  rights advocates protested outside the Texas Capitol in Austin in May, after the bill was signed. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Sergio Flores/Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210901eh9100031\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
