\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Appeal for Intervention On Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 698 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 August 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The law, set to go into effect on Wednesday, bans abortions after about six weeks and deputizes citizens to file suits to enforce it.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas asked the Supreme Court on Monday to block a state law banning abortions in the state as early as six weeks into pregnancy. The law, one of the most restrictive in the nation, is poised to go into effect on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court does not intervene, lawyers for the providers said, access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas could largely end. \u226\'00\'E2\u8364\'20\'AC\u175\'00\'AF\par \par \uc2 ''In less than two days, Texas politicians will have effectively overturned Roe v. Wade,'' said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , president of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which represents the providers along with other groups.\par \par \uc2 ''We have filed an emergency motion in the Supreme Court to block this law before clinics are forced to turn patients away,'' she said. ''Patients will have to travel out of state -- in the middle of a pandemic -- to receive constitutionally guaranteed health care. And many will not have the means to do so.''\par \par \uc2 In their application, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers wrote that the law ''would immediately and catastrophically reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Texas, barring care for at least 85 percent of Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients (those who are six weeks pregnant or greater) and likely forcing many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics ultimately to close.''\par \par \uc2 The Texas law is at odds with Supreme Court precedents, which prohibit states from banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability, at about 22 to 24 weeks. But it has an unusual feature complicating efforts to challenge it in court.\par \par \uc2 Instead of making state officials responsible for enforcing the law, it allows private citizens to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and others who help women obtain the procedure. That help can include financial assistance or giving a woman a ride to a clinic.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs need not claim any connection to those they sue. If they prevail, the law entitles them to damages of at least $10,000, along with legal expenses.\par \par \uc2 The law, the providers told the Supreme Court, ''attempts to replace normal civil-litigation rules and clearly established federal constitutional rules with distorted versions designed to maximize the abusive and harassing nature of the lawsuits and to make them impossible to fairly defend against.''\par \par \uc2 Taking state officials out of the equation complicated the providers' efforts to challenge the law in court, as such officials are the usual defendants in suits seeking to block laws. The providers instead also sued, among others, every state trial court judge and county court clerk in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The defendants argued that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers could challenge the constitutionality of the law only by violating it and raising their objections as defenses.\par \par \uc2 A federal trial judge rejected a motion to dismiss the case and scheduled a hearing on whether to block the law. But the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit, in New Orleans, canceled the hearing.\par \par \uc2 The challengers said that they are at minimum entitled to a decision on whether the law ought to be enjoined. If the Supreme Court does not act, they wrote, ''applicants and thousands of other Texans will be stripped of their fundamental constitutional rights on Wednesday without ever receiving a decision on their fully briefed request for a preliminary injunction.''\par \par \uc2 The Texas law is one of many efforts to limit abortions enacted in recent years in states controlled by Republicans. In 2021, state legislatures set the record for the most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions signed in a single year in the United States, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which compiles data on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statistics and supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has in the meantime become more conservative and now includes three members appointed by President Donald J. Trump, who had vowed to name justices prepared to overrule Roe v. Wade. The court will hear a major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case this fall concerning a Mississippi law that bans abortions after 15 weeks and amounts to a direct challenge to Roe.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \uc2  rights protested outside the Texas Capitol in Austin in May. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Sergio Flores/Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210831eh8v0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
