\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 How Covid Broadened Access To  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Bazelon \par \uc2 1,654 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 By Emily Bazelon\par \par \uc2 Good morning. During the pandemic, health care providers can send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication by mail. Will the courts allow that to continue?\par \par \uc2 How the pandemic changed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access\par \par \uc2 The challenges of the pandemic have resulted in new ways of providing care that reach beyond Covid-19 treatments and vaccines. One area of medicine that could see changes is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 A growing number of people seeking abortions in the U.S. -- almost 40 percent in 2017, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  -- take F.D.A.-approved pills. The regimen includes one drug to stop the pregnancy, and another that causes cramping and bleeding, like a miscarriage. For years, research has shown that this method is safe and more than 95 percent effective.\par \par \uc2 The increasing use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills is raising an important question for people in places with a dearth of providers: Do women need to go to a clinic or a doctor to take them, or to get an ultrasound beforehand, as the U.S. has long required? Because of Covid, researchers now have a clearer answer.\par \par \uc2 A study, led by Dr. Abigail Aiken at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 , took advantage of a natural pandemic experiment. In spring 2020, Britain began allowing health care providers to administer medication abortions via telemedicine, with pills mailed to the patient's home. Aiken and her colleagues compared thousands of medication abortions in Britain for two months before and after the new protocol went into effect. The groups had equally high success rates for completing their abortions (above 98 percent) and similarly low rates of significant complications (0.02 percent of the telemedicine-only abortions, and up to 0.04 percent for the ones with in-person visits). Other recent studies in the U.S. found similar results.\par \par \uc2 And yet telemedicine-only abortions are available only temporarily in the U.S. When the F.D.A. approved mifepristone, one \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug, in 2000, the agency imposed significant restrictions, requiring providers to obtain a special certification to stock the drug and to give it out only in a clinic, doctor's office or hospital.\par \par \uc2 For the moment, Covid has changed these rules. In response to a lawsuit by the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  and other groups, the F.D.A. said in April that mifepristone could be mailed to patients for the duration of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 A future before the court?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents called the F.D.A.'s decision ''irresponsible,'' arguing that in-person visits were needed to rule out medical risks and ensure the patient's full consent.\par \par \uc2 But those who want to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access are fighting to make the pandemic rules permanent. ''We're on the cusp of revolutionizing the provision of care,'' Destiny Lopez, co-president of the activist group All* Above All, told me.\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A. has the power to lift the old restrictions for good, and it is reviewing them. But even if the agency does so, Lopez said, ''we won't be there yet.'' Nineteen states effectively ban the use of telemedicine for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If the F.D.A. says the medical evidence justifies making the pills more accessible, a plaintiff could challenge a state telemedicine ban as lacking a real basis in science.\par \par \uc2 But the current Supreme Court, with its conservative majority, is more likely to cut back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access than to increase it, no matter what the science says. (In its next term, which opens in October, the court will hear another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, challenging Mississippi's near-total ban on abortions after 15 weeks.)\par \par \uc2 Court rulings and state laws can't stop people from taking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at home, because the medication is easily available online. So are consultations and information about how to use it. But where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by mail and telemedicine is illegal, it will remain underground. People could be prosecuted for aiding or participating in it, as a few have been.\par \par \uc2 A post-Roe America in which many more women take \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in the legal shadows wouldn't present the same dangers as the pre-Roe era. But it would pose its own risks.\par \par \uc2 Emily Bazelon is a writer for The New York Times Magazine.\par \par \uc2 Related:\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 A federal appeals court yesterday upheld a Texas law banning the most common form of second-trimester \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 THE LATEST NEWS\par \par \uc2 Afghanistan\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 The U.S. evacuated 2,000 people from Kabul in 24 hours, mostly Afghans and \uc2 NATO\uc2  personnel. Outside the airport, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  let some Afghans with visas and tickets enter but turned others away.\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 President Biden said troops could stay past his Aug. 31 withdrawal date if needed to assist with evacuations.\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fighters are searching for former members of the Afghan military and police, according to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 Afghanistan's former first vice president challenged the insurgents' legitimacy from a part of the country still outside \uc2 Taliban\uc2  control. Fighters dispersed protesters in two cities, an early sign of resistance, but protests continued on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 The U.S. blocked the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  from accessing billions of dollars belonging to the \uc2 Afghan central bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 \uc2 Ashraf Ghani\uc2 , the former president who fled Afghanistan, surfaced in the United Arab Emirates. He said he left to avoid being ''hanged.''\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 Coercion, persuasion and speed: How the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  seized Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 The Virus\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 The Biden administration took steps to deter states from banning masks in schools and push nursing home workers to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 Federal officials said Covid vaccines' protection against infection may wane over time, justifying booster shots. The vaccines remain highly effective at preventing hospitalizations and deaths.\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 Israel, despite its high vaccination rate, is nearing a peak in infections as the Delta variant spreads.\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 Gov. Greg Abbott of Texas attended a crowded, mostly maskless indoor event a day before he tested positive. Abbott, a Republican, is fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Other Big Stories\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 The E.P.A. will block the use of the pesticide chlorpyrifos on food crops. Studies have linked it to brain damage in children.\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 Tropical Storm Grace strengthened into a hurricane as it moved toward the Yucat\u195\'00\'C3\u161\'00\'A1n Peninsula. Another storm, Henri, could become a hurricane by Friday.\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 A cyberattack on \uc2 T-Mobile\uc2  exposed the names and social security numbers of over 40 million people.\par \par \uc2 \u226\'00\'E2\u8211\'20\'13\u160\'00\'A0 A court in Alaska blocked permits for an expansive oil drilling project, citing environmental reasons.\par \par \uc2 Opinions\par \par \uc2 ''No progress'': The New Yorker's David Rohde on trying to save the family of Tahir Luddin, an Afghan journalist who saved Rohde's life after the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  kidnapped both of them years ago.\par \par \uc2 MORNING READS\par \par \uc2 Summer reading: With a single word as their guide, sportswriters set out to find ''freedom.''\par \par \uc2 Attack of the superweeds: Agriculture may never be the same.\par \par \uc2 Real Housewives: It's Kathy Hilton's time in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 A Times classic: How to get over ghosting.\par \par \uc2 Lives Lived: Yasuhiro Wakabayashi, the Japanese American fashion photographer known as Hiro, transformed ordinary objects into something desirable. He died at 90.\par \par \uc2 ARTS AND IDEAS\par \par \uc2 R. Kelly's musical legacy\par \par \uc2 The trial of the R&B star R. Kelly began yesterday. He faces charges of racketeering based on sexual exploitation of children, kidnapping and forced labor.\par \par \uc2 Amid decades of allegations that Kelly has abused young women, which he denies, his musical legacy remains complicated. After the 2019 Lifetime documentary series ''Surviving R. Kelly,'' many D.J.s stopped playing his music. His songs, including hits like ''I Believe I Can Fly'' and ''Bump N' Grind,'' largely disappeared from radio stations. The documentary ''resonated so strongly that it just brought people to a place of never playing him,'' Peter Rosenberg, a D.J. and radio host, said.\par \par \uc2 But online, the popularity of his music has remained fairly steady, as Joe Coscarelli and Ben Sisario report. Assessing Kelly's performance across several streaming and social-media platforms, the data service Chartmetric ranks him as one of music's top 500 artists, at a level similar to those of Michael Bubl\u195\'00\'C3\u169\'00\'A9 and Carrie Underwood. Some of TikTok's most popular influencers have used his music to soundtrack their posts.\par \par \uc2 ''Such a dichotomy may be the fate of superstar entertainers accused of serious misconduct -- pariahs in certain places, but with enduring bodies of work that still draw large audiences,'' Coscarelli and Sisario write. Michael Jackson, the subject of another 2019 documentary alleging sexual abuse, remains steadily popular on the radio. Streams of his songs have actually grown. -- Sanam Yar, a Morning writer\par \par \uc2 PLAY, WATCH, EAT\par \par \uc2 What to Cook\par \par \uc2 Pair watermelon, which works beautifully in summer salads, with avocado and radish.\par \par \uc2 What to Listen to\par \par \uc2 How the National's Aaron Dessner found his voice (with the help of Taylor Swift).\par \par \uc2 What to Watch\par \par \uc2 The mean girls from \uc2 HBO\uc2 's ''The White Lotus'' explain themselves.\par \par \uc2 Late Night\par \par \uc2 Seth Meyers discussed the U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Now Time to Play\par \par \uc2 The pangram from yesterday's Spelling Bee was lexicon. Here is today's puzzle -- or you can play online.\par \par \uc2 Here's today's Mini Crossword, and a clue: Furry feet (four letters).\par \par \uc2 If you're in the mood to play more, find all our games here.\par \par \uc2 ______\par \par \uc2 Thanks for spending part of your morning with The Times. See you tomorrow. -- David\par \par \uc2 P.S. Julie Tate, a researcher who has contributed to Pulitzer Prize-winning stories at The Washington Post, is joining The Times's Investigations team.\par \par \uc2 Here's today's print front page.\par \par \uc2 ''The Daily'' is about Afghan interpreters the U.S. left behind. ''Sway'' features the Trump aide \uc2 Jason Miller\uc2 . And two Times journalists discuss the legacy of Jay-Z's ''Black Album.''\par \par \uc2 Isabella Kwai, Ian Prasad Philbrick, Tom Wright-Piersanti and Sanam Yar contributed to The Morning. You can reach the team at themorning@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Sign up here to get this newsletter in your inbox .\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210820eh8k00052\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
