\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Mississippi Asks Supreme Court to Reject Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 613 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 July 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The case, to be argued in the fall, will give the court's newly expanded conservative majority its first chance to consider the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Calling Roe v. Wade ''egregiously wrong,'' Mississippi's attorney general urged the Supreme Court on Thursday to do away with the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to sustain a state law that bans most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The court will hear arguments in the case in the fall, giving its newly expanded conservative majority a chance to confront what may be the most divisive issue in American law: whether the Constitution protects the right to end pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Lower courts blocked the Mississippi statute, calling it a cynical and calculated assault on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights squarely at odds with Supreme Court precedents. The justices agreed to hear the case in May, just months after Justice Amy Coney Barrett, who has said she personally opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , joined the court. She replaced Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , a proponent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, who died in September.\par \par \uc2 The new filing, from Attorney General Lynn Fitch, was a sustained and detailed attack on Roe and the rulings that followed it, notably Planned Parenthood v. Casey, the 1992 decision that said states may not impose an ''undue burden'' on the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability -- the point at which fetuses can sustain life outside the womb, or about 23 or 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 ''The Constitution does not protect a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Ms. Fitch wrote. ''The Constitution's text says nothing about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nothing in the Constitution's structure implies a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or prohibits states from restricting it.''\par \par \uc2 She told the justices that the scope of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights should be determined through the political process. ''The national fever on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can break only when this court returns \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to the states -- where agreement is more common, compromise is often possible and disagreement can be resolved at the ballot box.''\par \par \uc2 The law at issue in the case, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, No. 19-1392, was enacted in 2018 by the Republican-dominated Mississippi Legislature. It banned abortions if ''the probable gestational age of the unborn human'' was determined to be more than 15 weeks. The statute included narrow exceptions for medical emergencies or ''a severe fetal abnormality.''\par \par \uc2 The law was challenged by Mississippi's sole \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, which is represented by the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an advocacy group. The center's president, \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , said she was dismayed by the state's new filing.\par \par \uc2 ''Mississippi has stunningly asked the Supreme Court to overturn Roe and every other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights decision in the last five decades,'' Ms. Northup said in a statement. ''Today's brief reveals the extreme and regressive strategy, not just of this law, but of the avalanche of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and restrictions that are being passed across the country.''\par \par \uc2 The precise question the justices agreed to decide was ''whether all pre-viability prohibitions on elective abortions are unconstitutional.'' Depending on how the court answers that question, it could reaffirm, revise or do away with the longstanding constitutional framework for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fitch urged the justices to take the third approach, saying it would bolster the legitimacy of the court.\par \par \uc2 ''Roe and Casey are unprincipled decisions that have damaged the democratic process, poisoned our national discourse, plagued the law -- and, in doing so, harmed this court,'' she wrote.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\uc2  demonstrators outside the Supreme Court building in June. The court will hear an \uc2  rights case in the fall. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SARAHBETH MANEY/THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210723eh7n00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
