\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Judge Blocks Stringent Law On Abortions In Arkansas\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maria Cramer \par \uc2 897 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 July 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The law would have banned the procedure except to save the life of a pregnant woman in a medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge on Tuesday temporarily blocked Arkansas from enforcing a strict new law that would ban nearly all abortions, a decision that comes as many states with Republican-controlled legislatures are trying to force the issue before a newly reshaped Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Arkansas is one of several states that have passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions challenging the constitutional right to the procedure established in Roe v. Wade. Judges have temporarily blocked laws restricting abortions in states including Ohio, Arkansas and Texas.\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court overturned Roe, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be likely to become illegal in 22 states. In May, justices agreed to hear a case concerning a Mississippi law in the court's next term, giving the court's conservative majority an opportunity to place new constraints on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, U.S. District Judge Kristine G. Baker said that the Arkansas law, which would have banned abortions in all cases except to save the life of a pregnant woman in a medical emergency, would cause ''imminent irreparable harm'' to doctors and their patients.\par \par \uc2 The law ''is categorically unconstitutional, and plaintiffs have demonstrated that they are likely to succeed on the merits,'' wrote Judge Baker, who was appointed by President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The decision, by the U.S. District Court of the Eastern District of Arkansas, was made in a case brought by Little Rock Family Planning Services, Planned Parenthood Great Plains and Dr. Janet Cathey, a gynecologist and obstetrician in Little Rock, who was representing her staff and her patients.\par \par \uc2 In May, they filed a complaint against state officials who are charged with enforcing criminal laws and medical licensing penalties, including prosecutors in Pulaski County, members of the Arkansas State Medical Board, and the secretary of the Arkansas Department of Health.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Leslie Rutledge's office defended the state officials in the case. Amanda Priest, a spokeswoman for Ms. Rutledge, said the attorney general was ''disappointed'' by the decision and would review it ''to consider the next appropriate step.''\par \par \uc2 The law, which \uc2 Gov. Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , a Republican, signed in March and was scheduled to take effect on July 28, states that a ''person shall not purposely perform or attempt to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except to save the life of a pregnant woman in a medical emergency.''\par \par \uc2 The law defines ''medical emergency'' to mean ''a condition in which an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to preserve the life of a pregnant woman whose life is endangered by a physical disorder, physical illness, or physical injury, including a life-endangering physical condition caused by or arising from the pregnancy itself.''\par \par \uc2 Under the law, a doctor who performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have been punished with up to 10 years in prison or a fine of up to $100,000.\par \par \uc2 The language of the law explicitly calls on the Supreme Court to take up the issue, citing Roe and other cases that established a woman's constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''It is time for the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  to redress and correct the grave injustice and the crime against humanity which is being perpetuated by its decisions in Roe v. Wade, Doe v. Bolton, and Planned Parenthood v. Casey,'' the Arkansas law states.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Governor Hutchinson said he signed the law ''because of my sincere and long-held pro-life convictions.''\par \par \uc2 ''This legislation had the dual purpose of protecting Arkansas's unborn and challenging longstanding Supreme Court precedent regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' he said. ''I hope the Supreme Court will ultimately accept this case for review.''\par \par \uc2 Meagan Burrows, a staff attorney at the A.C.L.U. Reproductive Freedom Project and one of the lawyers who represented the plaintiffs, said in a statement that the law ''would disproportionately harm people of color, people who live in rural areas, and people with low incomes.''\par \par \uc2 Judge Baker wrote in her ruling that Arkansas is the fifth poorest state in the country and that Black women die as a result of childbirth ''at almost two times the rate of white women.''\par \par \uc2 The law, she wrote, would prohibit doctors from providing abortions ''unless and until the patient's condition deteriorates to such an extent that the very narrow 'medical emergency' exception is triggered.''\par \par \uc2 She wrote: ''This would pose serious risk to the physical, mental, and emotional health of these patients.''\par \par \uc2 Just a day after the ruling, another federal judge temporarily blocked Arkansas from enforcing a ban on gender-confirming treatments for transgender youths, a first-in-the-nation law that passed as the legislature overrode Mr. Hutchinson's veto.\par \par \uc2 Speaking about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling, Ms. Burrows said that the court's ruling ''should serve as a stark reminder to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politicians in Arkansas and other states that they cannot strip people of their right to make the deeply personal decision about whether to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or continue a pregnancy.''\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood Great Plains in Fayetteville, Ark., in 2018. The law, which Gov. Asa Hutchinson signed in March, says a ''person shall not purposely perform or attempt to perform an \uc2  except to save the life of a pregnant woman in a medical emergency.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY Nina Robinson for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210722eh7m0004q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
